1 OBJE
2 FORM URL
2 TITL Slektene Velde p.240
3 CONT Source: "Slektene Velde og Espeland og inngiftede slekter," History book (https://www.nb.no/items/bfd60befe5728733c5f9130b93ca7ce7 need Norway IP to see), by Berge Velde, 1957. p.240
3 CONT 
3 CONT Translation (errors within): 
3 CONT But the sources in the Middle Ages are so sparse on information that the family cannot be traced back with certainty further than to Filippus Erlendson. He was married to Ingebjørg Erlendsdatter, and it is assumed that he received Losnå farm wit
3 CONC h his wife. Both were still alive in 1338, when they gave consent for their daughter to have an illegitimate son she had with her cross-brother Haldor Jonson in Bergen.
3 CONT 
3 CONT In addition to their daughter Margrete, they also had a son, Erlend Filippusson of Losna, who is first mentioned in 1366 and is a councilor of the kingdom as early as 1369. He was with a number of Norway's other great men in negotiations that hav
3 CONC e been decisive for the history of Norway. Among other things, he was present at the negotiations in Kalundborg in 1376. In Icelandic annals it is said of him that he was Norway's strangest man.
3 CONT 
3 CONT He was a relative of Torgeir Torsteinson (Mita ?) and was his ombudsman in the inheritance of Gaute Håkonson in Tolga. See about this under Aga-Torstein Mita. He died in 1407. A daughter of Erlend Filipusson, Sigrid Erlendsdatter, was married to H
3 CONC åkon Sigurdson of Giske, Bjarkey and Slinde in Sogn, of the powerful family of the Havtorssønnenes, who were close relatives of the old royal house. Erlend's son, Eindride Erlendsson, became a knight in 1397 and soon after a member of the Counci
3 CONC l of State. He was one of Norway's most powerful men in the early 15th century. Like his father, he was involved in many decisions that have had an impact on our history. He was ombudsman for Finn Torgeirson in 1423. Mr. Eindride Erlandson had a so
3 CONC n, Mr. Erlend Eindrideson of Losna. About Mr. We know that Erlend was a prominent man who participated a lot in the country's government.
3 CONT 
3 CONT In 1450 he was among 20 other councilors who elected Christian I as Norwegian king. With him the male line at Losna became extinct, but he had two daughters who later owned the estate. His daughter Ingegerd was married to Mr. Olav Guttormson of Han
3 CONC anger at Lister. This marriage was childless. After Mr. Olav's death she was married to councilor Arild Kane, whom the farmers of Sunnmøre killed in 1496. The second daughter of Mr. Erlend was married to Bo Fleming of Nesøen in Asker, and from th
3 CONC eir daughter Margrete, who married Holger Rosenkrantz of Boller, the sect later comes through marriage in connection with the Rantzau and Thott families. More about these can be found in "Danmarks Adels Aarbog".
3 CONT 
3 CONT This was a little about the main branch of the Losnaætten, which belonged to the high nobility. As we have seen, Viking Finnson, who changed his name to Aga, has connections to this branch of the Losnaætten. Cf. the Aga line.
3 CONT 
3 CONT Another branch that is also supposed to belong to the Losna family, later settled at Semeleng in Valdres. This branch carried a coat of arms roughly similar to that of the Losna family and also bears the characteristic names Filippus and Fartegn
3 CONC . A judgment and genealogy about this branch has been reproduced in the NSH. for 1927, which provides a great deal of information about the family. It is believed that the royal councilor Erland Filippusson must have had a brother Fartegn, who is o
3 CONC therwise unknown, but his sons are supposed to be Fredrik and Filippus Fartegnsønner, who are mentioned in the judgment quoted below. Filippus has a son, Fartegn Filippusson who comes to Semeleng in Valdres, and from him, who was counted as part o
3 CONC f the lava family, we find the family in the 16th and 17th centuries again as noblemen and noble farmers in Ryfylke, Hordaland and Sogn. In the above-mentioned judgment extract it is stated:
3 CONT 
3 CONT ...
2 FILE https://sg30p0.familysearch.org/service/records/storage/dascloud/patron/v2/TH-7755-162927-374274-51/dist.png?ctx=ArtCtxPublic
1 OBJE
2 FORM URL
2 TITL Slektene Velde p.196
3 CONT source: "Slektene Velde og Espeland og inngiftede slekter," History book (https://www.nb.no/items/bfd60befe5728733c5f9130b93ca7ce7 need Norway IP to see), by Berge Velde, 1957. p.196 
3 CONT 
3 CONT Translation (has errors): In a letter from the Pope in 1351 he is called Ivarus de Oma - Ivar of Oma - and in this letter the Pope gives him and his wife, Ingrid, permission to choose their own confessor. Mrs. Ingrid is mentioned by Absalon Pederse
3 CONC n as Ingerid Torbjørnsdatter, but her lineage is not known.
3 CONT 
3 CONT At the council meeting at Båhus in 1353, Mr. Ivar Andresson meets with the archbishop and 11 other councilors. He is not mentioned later, and probably died some time after this meeting. Tradition tells that at Indre Oma there was a large smokehous
3 CONC e until 1848, and above the entrance door was carved "Ivar Ivarson Slede 1347". If the name Ivar Ivarson is correctly rendered, he must be the son of the knight and councilor Ivar Slede. But this Ivar Ivarson is otherwise unknown, and we also do no
3 CONC t know more about the Oma farm until about 100 years later. Then it is Holte Toreson who owns most of this old noble farm.
3 CONT 
3 CONT In "Norges beskrivelse" Absalon Pedersen mentions barons and knights in the time of Håkon V. and the knights who "were 1351" and here Mr. Ivar Andressøn is mentioned.
3 CONT 
3 CONT It has been guessed that Holte Toreson is the grandson of Holte Torgrimsson who in 1343 replaced Ivar Slede as a judge, and it is believed that Holte Torgrimsson was married to the knight's daughter. Which seems likely judging by the property deed
3 CONC s.
3 CONT 
3 CONT In 1452 Holte Toreson is seen to have married Gudrun Jonsdatter and gives her 18 laup bol in Oma as collateral for what she brought into the joint estate, and with the right to redeem it for 8 marks of gold. This letter was written in Norum in Sogn
3 CONC , and Holte's son Tore and daughter Gudrun, who was married to Svein Torgilsson Rustung, were present. Svein Rustung had a son, Eindride, who was the sole heir to his parents' estate.
3 CONT 
3 CONT Eindride Rustung was alderman in Bergen in 1479—1488, and from him the inheritance passed to his daughter, Sunniva. She was childless, and by a judgment in the council of the kingdom, her maternal sister, Herborg in Valen, was awarded all her est
3 CONC ate. Herborg was married to a man named Ivar, who probably lived in Valen in Kvinnherad. Ivar must have owned part of the farms Frosvik and Njos in Sogn. His son Gaute Ivarson in Valen sold 9 months' food in Frosvik and everything he owned in Njo
3 CONC s on October 6, 1521.
3 CONT 
3 CONT 
3 CONT In a court ruling from 1558, it is said that "Torben Olsen ÿ Quame" (Torbjørn Olavson Sandven?) was sued for estates that had once been sold to "Ivar Arneson in Wale"1) This is probably Herborg in Valen's husband and father of Gaute and Laurits I
3 CONC varssønner and Anna Ivarsdatter. Herborg Torbjørnsdatter is mentioned as a widow in 1504 and then living in Valen. Her son Gaute Ivarson later lived on this farm, and Laurits Ivarson stayed in Sandvik. Anna Ivarsdatter was possibly married to Jo
3 CONC n på Stillufseike in Vats. We have told about Haukåsgodset under Ile-Stillufseike, which Laurits Ivarson lost in a lord's court ruling in 1524 to Jon på Stillufseike. This family in Valen and Sandvik belonged to old Norwegian nobility, and a bra
3 CONC nch of the family was later called Slee or Slede, and the family has the right of inheritance to the farm Oma, which the knight Ivar Slede owned.
3 CONT 
3 CONT There can hardly be any doubt that there must be a direct lineage from the knight Ivar Slede and to Ivar or his wife Herborg at Valen in 1504. The name Slede and the noble estate in Oma must confirm this. Gaute Ivarson's father is quite certainly "
3 CONC Ivar Arneson in Wale". He owns land in Sogn and possibly originates from there. The descendants - a branch of it - are called Dall. After Gaute Ivarson comes Olav Bagge as owner of Valen. At least shortly after Gaute's death ...
