0 @NFS243@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT THIS ARTICLE WAS PRINTED IN THE NORWEGIAN BIOGRAPHICAL LEXICON , PUBLISHED 1999-2005. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Born about 1315 in Norway; exact year of birth and place of birth are not known
1 CONT Sigurd Havtoresson belonged to the top tier of the Norwegian main man group in the 1300s and was at that time Norway's largest private landowner.
1 CONT 
1 CONT As daughters of Håkon 5, the Havtoresses had a background as no other Norwegian great men. After the father inherited a large amount of land, which was changed so that Sigurd took over his father's seat guard Sørum (Sudrheim) and the weight of th
1 CONC e land estate on Romerike, Jon got his body laid for Borgarsysla, concentrated on the big garden Huseby in Onsøy and Borregård in Tune.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The estate property increased through inheritance, made possible by the development after the Black Death. This brought two larger collections of goods to the Seashore Sons: whole or part of the estate the knight Sigurd Erlendsson at Sandbu in Våg
1 CONC å and his wife Gudrun Ivarsdotter - sad daughter of Ivar Jonsson, uncle of the Seashore Sons - died from (settlement 1353), and the estate collection to the sibling's sister Herdis , ma with property judges at Shetland (judgment 1366). On the othe
1 CONC r hand, it does not appear that they got any of the inheritance of Ogmund Finnsson, joking about Mr Ogmund after his own statements did not acknowledge heirs other than Mr Jon and Mr Sigurd.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1342 Sigurd was granted papal exemption to marry Virgo Ingebjørg, daughter of Erling Vidkunnsson; exemption throngs since they were to blame in the fourth paragraph. Erling's only son Bjarne died during or just after the Black Death, and when E
1 CONC rling passed away in 1355, Sigurd and Mrs. Ingebjørg were chief heirs. The head guard Giske had transferred Erling to Ingebjørg the year before, his son Bjarne had asked him to do so while he was lying lately. With the Giskegodset, they followe
1 CONC d the Northern Norwegian estates that Erling Vidkunnsson left behind, with the old big-bardies Bjarkøy, Tjøtta and Dønnes as centers. From now on Sigurd Havtoresson was Norway's most wealthy man, and Giske was the main guard in the estate comple
1 CONC x.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sigurd was in the crown of the men who rebelled against King Magnus in 1333, when he could hardly have been more than 18 years old, and 1338-39. Erling Vidkunnsson was the leader in the first uprising, in 1338-39 Sigurd was the principal man with I
1 CONC var Ogmundsson and Bjarne Erlingsson. A pope's letter in 1334 states that Sigurd was instrumental in taking Bishop Hallvard in Hamar to capture and rob him of goods and a castle. He was wanted in a curse, but released from the curse. It is unclea
1 CONC r whether the controversy was politically motivated.
1 CONT 
1 CONT After Atonement 1339, there is no direct evidence of conflict between the King and Sigurd, but things suggest that King Magnus in the first time kept Sigurd somewhat at a distance. At Varberg Castle in 1343, when King Magnus made junker Håkon th
1 CONC e Norwegian king, Sigurd, Bjarne Erlingsson and Ogmund Finnsson were present with weapons status. Four years later, when the will of King Magnus and Queen Blanca was set up, Ogmund had a knight, and Sigurd is placed second in the arms, after Bjarne
1 CONC . Sigurd and Bjarne must have received the knightly battle just after the will was set up (between 15.7.1347 and 16.2.1348). For Sigurd, this may have happened at the same time as he was a teammate in Oslo (named first team as teammate 18.8.1347)
1 CONC . The brother Jon about two years older then had our knights for 10 years. The Ombudsman had Sigurd threatened to say something after 1350,
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sigurd's strong cargo interests in Western Norway and Northern Norway can explain that he took over the prestigious position as head of Bergenhus. He was in open conflict with the Hanseatic 1365 and 1366, and in 1370 Hanseatic abuse against Mr Sigu
1 CONC rd is mentioned. This year, he was detained in Oslo, and during the plague that ravaged the city, after all, Mrs. Ingebjørg died. Truleg was Sigurd then a magistrate of Romerike; he had a sheriff here in 1376.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In the following period, it appears that Sigurd concentrated on the administration of the heavy collection of goods, which was heavily affected during the crisis after the Black Death. But he had an advanced place in the king's council. In the peac
1 CONC e treaty with Hanseatane in 1369, he is the only major man by the King's seat named as the king's adviser, and in several contexts, among other things, in 1381 when the king of the children Olav 4 paid tribute to the Øyratinget, he is referred t
1 CONC o as the king's kinsman. On the other hand, he never managed to get beaten. A letter from 1390, which called for a renewal of an earlier earthenware gift, should preferably be interpreted so that Sigurd was then dead.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sigurd Havtoresson and wife Ingebjørg left behind two sons, son Håkon, who died without heirs in 1407, and daughter Agnes. She was married to the Swedish-born Jon Marteinsson, and it was their son, Deputy Governor Sigurd Jonsson, who was sideline
1 CONC d with the heavy earthenware collection after his grandfather.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sources and literature
1 CONT The Akershus Register of 1622, 1916
1 CONT DN, vols 1-8, 13-14 and 19
1 CONT Isl.Ann.
1 CONT NgL, vol. 3 and rk. 2, vol. 1, 1904-12
1 CONT RN, vols 4-7
1 CONT G. Munthe: “Heraldic-Historical Records,” in SNFSH, vol. 4, 1836, pp. 128-131
1 CONT NFH, 2nd Division, Part 1, 1862
1 CONT NHfNF, vol. 2: 2, 1916
1 CONT H. Sollied: “Source Critical Studies on Some Medieval Genres. III. The family of Sudrheim, ”in NST, vol. 8, 1942, pp. 112-132, 259-280, and 384-402
1 CONT H. Koht: biography in NBL1, vol. 13, 1958
1 CONT G. Authén Blom: Norway in the Union in the 1300s, vol. 1-2, 1992
1 CONT H. Bjørkvik: “Ogmund Finnsson to Hestbø and the legacy after him”, in Ætt og heim 2001, 2002, pp. 7-34
1 CONT Portraits etc.
1 CONT Seal
1 CONT In letters from 1343 ( DN, vol. 2 no. 258) and 1355 ( DN, vol. 1 no. 345), Sigurd Havtoresson used two different seals. In addition, he used his own seal as a layman in Oslo 1347 ( DN, vol. 1 no. 307) and 1348 ( DN, vol. 4 no. 334). See HJ Huitfeld
1 CONC t-Kaas and others: Norske Sigiller from the Middle Ages, 1899/1950, Nos. 453, 629 and 526 (Sailor's Seal)
