0 @NFS706@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT "De tidigaste kända representanterna för släkten är två stormän som nämns i ett av kung Johan Sverkersson 1219 utfärdat brev, nämligen Mats och hans son Bengt Matsson. Båda var då redan döda och ingen av dem kallades Boberg."
1 CONT 
1 CONT "The earliest known representatives of the family are two big men mentioned in a letter issued by King Johan Sverkersson in 1219, namely Mats and his son Bengt Matsson. Both were then already dead and neither of them was called Boberg."
1 CONT 
1 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boberg_(fr%C3%A4lse%C3%A4tt)#Historia
0 @NFS707@ NOTE [google translation of portions of article content]Who was the builder of the church? The philologist Hallvard Magerøy explained in an article in 1988 the connection between Urnes church, the Orness family and the Ornes estate. There he pro
1 CONC ved that Gaut af Órnesi, who is mentioned in Sverre's saga, was the man behind the church building. Gaut lived in the middle of the 12th century and was a bagler. He had at least two sons who lived, Jon and Munan, who both took part in the civil w
1 CONC ars and who must have been "in their prime", i.e. around 30, in 1180 (Magerøy, 1988, 130). Magerøy based his assumption about Gaut as the builder on the fact that the current church building could be dated to the 1160s or 1170s (Magerøy, 1988, 1
1 CONC 24). Later, year ring dating has shown that Urnes church was built just after 1130. Then it is more likely that it was Gaut's father who was the builder of Urnes church, a man who has not left any name in the written sources. In this article, I sta
1 CONC rt from the hypothesis that Gaut's father, Gaut himself, Jon Gautsson and Jon's son Arnbjørn with their families made up the Orness family in the century from when the church was built until around 1230.
1 CONT ****
1 CONT As mentioned, Urne's stave church is dated to the years immediately after 1130. At that time, King Sigurd Jorsalfare had just died, and Norway entered a period of transition in both the literal and figurative sense. The succession to the throne aft
1 CONC er the king was not clear, and civil war quickly broke out. Eventually, the Ornes family would become involved in this dispute. But already when the church was under construction, it can be argued that it was an expression of the social changes tha
1 CONC t characterized 12th-century Norway.
1 CONT ***
1 CONT Let us now return to the family at Ornes, with Gaut, his sons Jon and Munan and grandson Arnbjørn Jonsson. Sverre's saga tells that the brothers Jon and Munan Gautsson were captains of one or two large warships in King Magnus Erlingsson's fleet. J
1 CONC on probably died in the battle of Nordnes in 1181. Then his son, Arnbjørn, had to take over the farm and estate. Hallvard Magerøy assumes that Arnbjørn was born in the 1170s, in which case he must have only been a small boy when his father died
1 CONC . He was sent to be brought up by Nikolas Arnesson from Stårheim in Nordfjord, who became bishop of Oslo in 1190 (Magerøy, 1988, 131). Nikolas belonged to the country's highest political elite, and being brought up by him was to have a major impa
1 CONC ct on the course of Arnbjørn's life.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Bishop Nikolas was indeed central when Erling Steinvegg (1204-1207) was elected as bagler king in 1204. His foster son Arnbjørn then chose to join King Erling, and from his base in the Oslofjord he made several raids against the Birkebeiners, wh
1 CONC o had gained a foothold in Western Norway. It may seem that Arnbjørn was a gifted and competent man who quickly gained central positions. He eventually became a close friend of the bagler king Filippus (1207-1217), and was second-in-command unde
1 CONC r him. When Filippus died, however, the Baglers had to admit that they had lost, and they took Håkon Håkonsson the Birkebeiner as king. Arnbjørn Jonsson then went into the new king's service, and from 1218 he was King Håkon's loyal supporter. T
1 CONC he king made him governor of Borgarsyssel and he had a fort built on Valdisholm (Magerøy, 1988, 132). We know that Arnbjørn took part in the battle of Låke in Nannestad in the spring of 1240; there the king's party suffered defeat and Arnbjør
1 CONC n died shortly afterwards. "These thick people were a great loss, because everyone agreed that at that time there was no landman in Norway who could measure up to him" ("Håkon Håkonsson's saga", chapter 226. Noregs kongesoger 4, 1979, 214).
1 CONT ***
1 CONT My hypothesis is that the inventory in the small Høgende church can very largely be linked to Ornesätten's heyday in the 12th century. Gaut's father built the church and acquired the calvary group around 1130. Perhaps he was also the man who orig
1 CONC inally enthroned the chair that now stands in the choir. Son Gaut and grandson Jon brought with them the Madonna figure with altarpiece, the monumental male saint and the "monk's head". Jon's son Arnbjørn may not have stayed much at Ornes after h
1 CONC e became an adult, but may be the man who procured the Limoges stakes for the church building. Hallvard Magerøy speculates whether King Sverre may have confiscated the Ornesgodset after the battle of Fimreite in 1184, and that King Håkon Håkonss
1 CONC on may have given it back to Arnbjørn. It is an interesting theory, but cannot be proven. If that was the case, the candlesticks may have been given to the church in connection with the re-establishment of Gaut's family on the church site. Then i
1 CONC t could also be that the repainting, or the restoration, of the calvary group in Urnes was carried out to mark that Arnbjørn Jonsson, son of Jon Gautsson, was lord of Ornes again.
1 CONT ***
1 CONT Who took over Ornesgodset after Arnbjørn? Neither the saga nor the Icelandic annals, which also tell of Arnbjørn's death, tell of any heirs of the great man. Magerøy believes that the estate may have already disappeared from the family's possess
1 CONC ion when Arnbjørn died (Magerøy, 1988, 139). A couple of generations later, around the year 1300, it was Norway's richest and most powerful man, Baron Bjarne Erlingsson of Bjarkøy, who owned Ornes. But at that time it was clear that Ornes was n
1 CONC o longer a place to live, at least not if your name was Bjarne Erlingsson and you were a royal adviser. In his extensive will we see that he gave spiritual gifts to dozens of churches and monasteries, but nothing to Urnes. Bjarne Erlingsson staye
1 CONC d primarily in Bergen, but was buried in Nidaros Cathedral in 1313. In his will from 1308 or 1309, Ornes is only mentioned as an estate (DN XV no. 155). In the middle of the 15th century, it was the well-known nobleman Hartvig Krummedike who owne
1 CONC d Ornes. He was staying in Eastern Norway; then Ornes was still a very large farm, but no longer a bastion of power.
0 @NFS708@ NOTE discusses runic inscriptions at the Urnes Stave Church, which indicate that “Gaut på Ænes” and family are connected to the Ornes Church (and thus to Ornes in Stryn and not Ænes in Kvinnherad); Gaut på Ornes and (son?) Jon are mention
1 CONC ed in the inscriptions. Magerøy surmises that Ornes got transcribed as Ænes by the scholar P. A. Munch in the 19th century of Sverre’s Saga. This in turn provides strong evidence that Gaut på Ornes are not the same family as Gaut på Ænes/Mel
1 CONC /Hatteberg and family. This in turn disproves the genealogical theories of Sollied (1930) and Høyland (1939) regarding the history of the ‘Galtung’ family.
0 @NFS709@ NOTE lendermann Jon Gautesen Ornes
1 CONT 
1 CONT Født: Ornes (Urnes), Luster, Sogn og fjordane
1 CONT Ekteskap: Ukjent
1 CONT Død: 1181, Nordnes, Bergen
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jon og broren Munan var også lendermenn, som faren. Og som de fleste av storættene på den tiden var også de med Magnus Erlingsen i striden mot kong Sverre. Både Jon og Munan er nevnt som skipssjefer i slaget ved Nordnes i Bjørgvin 1181. Og de
1 CONC t er sannsynlig at Jon fikk sin bane der, for etter slaget hører man ikke noe mer til ham, mens Munan fremdeles var en av Magnus sine menn. I slaget ved Norefjorden i Sogn i 1184 der Magnus Erlingsen falt, var det Munan som styrte skipet "Reinen"
1 CONC . Han er ikke særskilt nevnt blant de høvdingene som falt sammen med Magnus, men vi hører ikke noe til ham senere, så det er vel mest trolig at han fikk sin bane der, han også, mener Rasmus Høyland i "Gamle ætter i Sunnhordland".
1 CONT Odd Handegård peker i Vår felles slektshistorie s. 180 på at det "er sannsynlig at Munan overlevde de blodige slagene ved Nordnes (1181) og ved Fimreite (1184), men ingen etterkommere er nevnt etter ham. Også Jon deltok i slaget ved Nordnes i 1
1 CONC 181 der omlag 360 mann ble drept på begge sider i dette slaget mellom Magnus Erlingson og Sverre. Ettersom Jon ikke er nevnt i sagalitteraturen etter 1181, har det vært antatt at han ble drept ved Nordnes. Noen dokumentasjon ut over dette finne
1 CONC s imidlertid ikke."
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jon giftet seg med.
