0 @NFS807@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Haakon IV Haakonsson, reigning from 1217 to 1263, is known for consolidating the power of the Norwegian monarchy and ushering in a golden age of culture and stability in medieval Norway.
1 CONT Early Life and Ascension to the Throne
1 CONT Haakon IV was born around 1204, the illegitimate son of Haakon III. His early life was marked by the civil wars that plagued Norway during this period. After the death of King Inge II in 1217, Haakon was proclaimed king at the young age of 13, supp
1 CONC orted by the Birkebeiner faction. His legitimacy was initially questioned, leading to a trial by ordeal for his mother, Inga of Varteig, to prove his lineage. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Reign and Achievements
1 CONT Haakon IV's reign is often regarded as the beginning of Norway's "golden age." He successfully consolidated royal power, defeating rival factions, including the Baglers, and eliminating threats from within, such as Earl Skule Bårdsson, who was kil
1 CONC led in 1240 after attempting to usurp the throne. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Under his rule, Norway expanded its territories, gaining sovereignty over Iceland and Greenland in 1261-62. This expansion marked the peak of the Norwegian realm. Haakon also established significant trade treaties, including one with Henry III of E
1 CONC ngland and another with the German city of Lübeck, enhancing Norway's economic standing. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Cultural Contributions
1 CONT Haakon IV was a patron of the arts, promoting the translation of European literature into Old Norse and commissioning works that enriched Norwegian culture. His reign saw the construction of monumental stone buildings, reflecting the influence of c
1 CONC ontemporary European styles. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Legacy
1 CONT Haakon IV's legacy is characterized by his diplomatic skills and efforts to maintain peace and stability in Norway. He died in 1263 while preparing to defend Norwegian territories in the Scottish Isles against King Alexander III of Scotland. His re
1 CONC ign left a lasting impact on Norway, setting a precedent for future monarchs and contributing to the development of a more centralized and powerful monarchy. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT In summary, Haakon IV is remembered as a significant figure in Norwegian history, whose reign marked a period of cultural flourishing and territorial expansion, establishing a strong foundation for the future of the Norwegian state.
0 @NFS808@ NOTE Haakon had three illegitimate children with his mistress Kanga the Younger av Folkindberg (who is only known by name) (1198–1225), before 1225.[6] They were:[61]
1 CONT 
1 CONT (1) Sigurd (died 1252).[61]
1 CONT (2) Cecilia (died 1248). Married lendmann Gregorius Andresson, a nephew of the last Bagler king Philip Simonsson in 1241. Widowed in 1246, she married Harald Olafsson, King of Mann and the Isles in 1248. They both drowned the same year on the retur
1 CONC n voyage to Great Britain.[36]
1 CONT (3) Kone (c. 1225 -), married Toralde Gunnarsson Hvite, until Gulsvik (Buskerud, c. 1220 – Gulsvik, Flå, Buskerud, d. 1260), mentioned on a 1258 document, and had issue.
