1 CONT Eric IV – Murdered Danish King c. 1216-1241
1 CONT On the 10th of August 1250, Eric IV – also known as Eric Ploughpenny – was murdered on a boat and dumped in the firth of Schlei. His death was the culmination of ten years of civil war and the harbinger of an even more tumultuous period in th
1 CONC e medieval history of Denmark. https://www.medieval.eu/eric-iv-murdered-danish-king-c-1216-1241/
0 @NFS820@ NOTE St Bendt's Kirke, Ringsted, Region Zealand, Danmark
1 CONT STANDARDIZED PLACE NAME
0 @NFS821@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Eric was the second son of King Valdemar II of Denmark (1170-1241) and his Portuguese queen, Berenguela (1197-1221). At the age of seven, his father and older brother were taken captives by the German Count Henry of Schwerin during a hunt organise
1 CONC d at Lyø in May 1223.  According to a peace treaty between the Holy-Roman Emperor and Valdemar from 1214, the latter was the liege lord of Count Henry. Contemporary accounts could tell that the count had even taken part in the dinner in the tent t
1 CONC he night before. Hence, the event was regarded as shameless, and the Pope tried to intervene.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Nevertheless, the King and his son, Valdemar the Younger, were held for ransom, first at Lenzen in Brandenburg and later in the Castle of Dannenberg. The 33-metre high keep was built around 1200. Today and in memory of the two captives, the tower i
1 CONC s still known as The “Waldemarturm”. With its location far to the southeast, at the brink of the river Elb, the damp walls of the keep measured 3,5 metres, and one chronicle tells us the prison was “hard”. Were they chained? Probably not. S
1 CONC uch hostages were “valuable” goods.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Whatever the conditions, negotiations were long and harsh. Although the king was released inside a year, his heir did not return until three years later, when Eric and his brother Abel were given as hostages until the enormous sum of 45.000 mark si
1 CONC lver had been paid. (Perhaps the equivalent of a three to four years of income from the minting in Denmark at this time [1].  Also, Valdemar was pledged to surrender the principalities along the Northern coast of Germany between the rivers Elb an
1 CONC d Eider, which he had captured and held in 1214. Further, numerous minor gifts and treasures were supposed to be paid – 100 horses, red cloth to mount a hundred knights plus the queen’s gold. Not quite in the same league as the ransom paid fo
1 CONC r Richard Lionheart, which amounted to 150.000 marks; and yet of immense concern for the future of the kingdom of Denmark. Probably, the ransom was a major reason for the drastic overhaul of the royal finances, which took place after the return o
1 CONC f the King.
0 @NFS822@ NOTE Tanberg is the medieval farm name.
1 CONT Tandberg is the modernized spelling that became common in later centuries and in genealogical databases.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Medieval Norwegian spelling was not fixed, so variants like Tanberg, Tornberg, Tonberg, and Tandberg all appear in records and later genealogical tradition.
1 CONT Alv lived ca. 1190–1240, well before the period when ‑sen endings became dominant (14th–17th centuries). In the saga era and early medieval period, the ‑son / ‑datter forms were universal.
0 @NFS823@ NOTE Standarization without changing data
0 @NFS824@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Alv Erlingsen Tandberg was born around 1190 at Tornberg/Tonberg in Ringebu, Oppland, into a lineage already tied to land, status, and regional authority. His father, Erling “the Old” Torneberg, appears in the record as a landholder of standing
1 CONC , and Alv inherited both position and responsibility in a Norway still shaped by shifting alliances and the consolidation of royal power.
1 CONT 
1 CONT By adulthood, Alv held the rank of lendermann—a noble title often equated with jarl or earl—marking him as one of the king’s trusted regional magnates. His authority included the forlening (feudal grant) of Borgarsyssel, a strategically impor
1 CONC tant district. This grant had passed to him from his father, reinforcing the family’s longstanding influence. Upon Alv’s death, the estate reverted to the crown, though it later returned to private ownership and was eventually divided into seve
1 CONC ral holdings around 1500.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Around 1210, Alv married Ingebjørg Bårdsdatter at Tangen in Buskerud, forming a union that linked two noble families. Together they raised a sizeable household—five children are recorded—including Erling Alvsson Tanberg, who continued the fam
1 CONC ily’s noble line, and Audun Alvsson Råde, remembered with the same lendermann title.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Alv’s life unfolded during a period of political tension and intermittent conflict in medieval Norway. Notes preserved in the FamilySearch profile indicate he was “drept” (killed), suggesting his death in 1240 at Tanberg in Norderhov may hav
1 CONC e been violent—an end not uncommon for nobles navigating the era’s factional struggles.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Though the surviving record is concise, Alv emerges as a figure rooted in land, lineage, and regional authority: a nobleman whose family shaped the history of Borgarsyssel and whose descendants carried the Tandberg name into later generations.
