0 @NFS932@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Ingebjørg Bårdsdatter was born around 1190 at Reinsnes in Nordland, a daughter of Lendermann Bård Skúle Guttormsson Rein and Ragnhild Erlingsdatter, members of a powerful noble family whose influence stretched across Norway’s political landsc
1 CONC ape. She grew up within the Rein kin group, a lineage that produced several prominent figures, including her brother Duke Skule Bårdsson, one of the most consequential nobles of the early 13th century.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Around 1210, Ingebjørg married Alv Erlingsson of Tanberg, a lendermann and regional magnate. Their marriage joined two influential families—Rein in the north and Tanberg in Buskerud—strengthening political alliances during a period marked by s
1 CONC hifting loyalties and intermittent conflict. Together they raised a household of five children, including Erling Alvsson Tanberg and Audun Alvsson Råde, both remembered as noblemen in their own right.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Ingebjørg spent her later years at Tanberg in Norderhov, where she died around 1250. Her life reflects the world of Norway’s medieval aristocracy: a network of landholding families whose marriages, alliances, and responsibilities shaped regiona
1 CONC l governance and the emerging national story.
