0 @NFS1042@ NOTE Jutta af Sachsen (født omkring 1223, død 1267) var en dansk dronning, gift med Erik Plovpenning i 1239. Hun var datter af hertiug Albrecht 1. af Sachsen og Agnes af Østrig.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jutta giftede sig den 9. oktober (alternativt 17. november) 1239 med daværende danske medkonge og tronarving Erik Valdemarssøn. Da Juttas svigefar kong Valdemar Sejr var enkemand, siden hans anden dronnings død i 1221 så blev Jutta enedronnin
1 CONC g i Danmark ved sit giftermål. Hendes mand Erik Plovpenning blev først selv enekonge af Danmark ved Valdemar Sejrs død to år senere i 1241.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jutta er kendt for sin konflikt med munkene i Øm kloster, fra hvem hun konfiskerede deres korn for sine egne ejendomme. Hun underskrev også ægtefællens ønske om at blive begravet klædt som en munk.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Efter sin ægtefælles død blev Jutta gift igen med grev Burchard 7. af Querfurt-Rosenburg (i Magdeburg).[1]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Børn:
1 CONT Sofia Eriksdatter af Danmark, gift med Valdemar Birgersson af Sverige.
1 CONT Jutta af Danmark
1 CONT Agnes af Danmark
1 CONT Ingeborg af Danmark, norsk dronning, gift med Magnus Lagabøter.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Noter:
1 CONT 1. Gunnar Carlquist: Svensk uppslagsbok, Bd 14; Malmö 1933; side 761
1 CONT 
1 CONT Denne side blev senest ændret den 26. juni 2020 kl. 23:15.
0 @NFS1043@ NOTE Jutta of Saxony (c. 1223 – before 2 February 1267) was a Danish Queen consort, spouse of King Eric IV of Denmark. Jutta was the daughter of Albert I, Duke of Saxony. She married king Erik in 1239, and became junior queen consort, since he
1 CONC r husband was junior king, even though there were no senior queen at the time. She would become senior queen in 1242.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Not many details are known about Queen Jutta. Jutta was involved in a conflict with the monks of Øm Abbey, from whom she confiscated corn from their estates and shipped it to her own. Her signature was also on the instruction regarding the funera
1 CONC l service of her spouse, in which he expressed the wish to be buried in the clothes of a monk. She was queen for eight years and widowed in 1250 when her spouse was murdered. She is believed to have returned to Saxony as a widow, leaving her daught
1 CONC ers in Denmark.[1]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jutta married a second time and became the first wife of Count Burchard VIII of Querfurt-Rosenburg, who held function and title of Burgrave of Magdeburg (recorded between 1273 and 1313), a son of Burgrave Burchard VI (recorded between 1221 and 1273
1 CONC ).[2]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Her children with Eric IV were:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sophia of Denmark, (1241–1286) married to King Valdemar of Sweden
1 CONT Canute (Knud) of Denmark (b. & d. 1242)
1 CONT Ingeborg of Denmark (1244–1287), married to King Magnus VI of Norway
1 CONT Jutta of Denmark, prioress St. Agnes' Priory, Roskilde (1246–1284)
1 CONT Christof of Denmark (b. & d. 1247)
1 CONT Agnes of Denmark, prioress of St. Agnes' Priory, Roskilde (1249–1288/95)
1 CONT Her daughter with Burchard VIII was:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sophia Burghardsdatter von Querfurt-Rosenburg (d. 1325), married with Erik Erikssøn Langben (1272–1310), Duke of Langeland, son of Eric I, Duke of Schleswig
1 CONT 
1 CONT References:
1 CONT Alf Henrikson: Dansk historia (Danish history) (1989) (Swedish)
1 CONT Sven Rosborn (In Swedish): När hände vad i Nordens historia (When did what happen in the historiy of the Nordic countries) (1997)
1 CONT Dansk biografisk Lexikon / VIII. Bind. Holst - Juul (in Danish)
1 CONT 
