0 @NFS1076@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Life Sketch
1 CONT 
1 CONT Kristina Håkonsdatter was a Norwegian princess, born about 1234 as a daughter of King Håkon IV Håkonsson of Norway and his queen, Margrete Skulesdatter. She grew up within the Norwegian royal court during a period of consolidation and increase
1 CONC d contact between Norway and the wider European world.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In the late 1250s, the Castilian king Alfonso X sought to strengthen diplomatic ties with Norway through marriage. Kristina was chosen to travel from Norway to Castile, a journey that took her through several European courts and was remembered in l
1 CONC ater tradition for its length and ceremony. In Castile she eventually married Alfonso’s younger brother, Infante Felipe of Castile, a royal prince who had previously pursued an ecclesiastical career before entering into this dynastic marriage.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Kristina’s marriage symbolized the alliance between the Norwegian and Castilian monarchies, but the union produced no known surviving children. She lived the final years of her life in Spain, far from her homeland. Kristina died in 1262, and late
1 CONC r traditions connect her memory to a church in Covarrubias, where a medieval tomb associated with her became the focus of local remembrance.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Because she married into the Castilian royal family, Kristina is often called “Cristina de Noruega” in Spanish‑language sources. In Norwegian and English historical writing, she is more accurately referred to as Kristina Håkonsdatter or Prin
1 CONC cess Kristina of Norway, reflecting the patronymic naming customs of medieval Norway.
1 CONT 
1 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina_of_Norway,_Infanta_of_Castile
1 CONT https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cristina_de_Noruega
1 CONT https://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kristina_av_Tunsberg
1 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kristina_H%C3%A5kansdotter
1 CONT 
1 CONT `
