0 @NFS1443@ NOTE In 1260, Valdemar married Sophia, the eldest daughter of King Eric IV of Denmark and Jutta of Saxony. Valdemar also had a relationship with his sister-in-law Jutta. In 1272, Princess Jutta visited Sweden and became Valdemar's mistress. Th
1 CONC e affair resulted in a child born in 1273. The following year, Jutta was placed in a convent and Valdemar was forced to make a pilgrimage to Rome to ask for the absolution of the Pope.
1 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valdemar,_King_of_Sweden
0 @NFS1444@ NOTE https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valdemar,_King_of_Sweden
0 @NFS1445@ NOTE GEDCOM data
0 @NFS1446@ NOTE GEDCOM data
0 @NFS1447@ NOTE Sophia was the eldest daughter of Eric IV of Denmark and Jutta of Saxony. She had no living brothers because the two of them died young, but had three surviving sisters" Ingeborg, Agnes and Jutta. Her father was murdered in 1250 when she an
1 CONC d her sisters were children. As he left no son, he was succeeded by his brother Abel, and then in 1252 by his second brother Christopher I.
1 CONT The marriage between Sophia of Denmark and King Valdemar of Sweden was arranged as a part of the policy of peaceful diplomacy between Denmark and Sweden which was favored by Birger Jarl, Valdemar's father and the de facto Regent of Sweden.[1] In 12
1 CONC 54, Birger Jarl had acted as a mediator between Christopher I and King Haakon of Norway, and when Christopher I needed support against Archbishop Jakob Erlandsen of Lund, an alliance was made between Christopher I, King Haakon of Norway and Birge
1 CONC r Jarl in 1258. In connection to this, Christopher I engaged his niece Sophia to Birger's son Valdemar of Sweden, and his niece Ingeborg to Haakon's son Magnus VI of Norway.[1] A dispensation from the Pope was necessary to allow a marriage within t
1 CONC he degree of affinity, which was granted by Alexander IV 1 March 1259 with the motivation that Swedes and Danes would through this marriage be more able to fight against their neighboring Pagans.[1] In Erikskrönikan it is said that when she was in
1 CONC formed about the arranged marriage, she left the room, went into her chamber and asked the Virgin Mary: "Give me happiness with him and him with me."[2]
1 CONT [1]  Sophia, urn:sbl:6154, Svenskt biografiskt lexikon (article by Dick Harrison), retrieved 2016-09-06.
1 CONT [2] Öhman, Christer (1994). Helgon, bönder och krigare: berättelser ur den svenska medeltidens historia [Saints, farmers and warriors. Stories from Sweden's medieval history] (in Swedish). Stockholm: Rabén Prisma. ISBN 9789151826677.
0 @NFS1448@ NOTE 1. Ingeborg (1263–1292), countess of Holstein, married to Gerhard II, Count of Holstein-Plön
1 CONT 2. Eric (1272–1330), riksråd, Danish throne claimant in 1328–29.
1 CONT 3. Marina, married in 1285 to Rudolf, Count of Diepholz
1 CONT 4. Richeza (d. c. 1292), Queen of Poland, married in 1285 to Przemysł II of Poland
1 CONT 5. Catherine (d. 1283)
1 CONT 6. Margaret (d. after 1288), nun at Skänninge Abbey from 1288
