0 @NFS2448@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Bjørn Ulvhedinsen Kamp was born around 1220 at Holter in Nes, Akershus, a region of clustered farms and early noble landholders. He grew up in a Norway still shaped by the aftermath of the civil war era, where stability depended on strong familie
1 CONC s managing land, labor, and local obligations. As the senior male of the Kamp line in the mid‑13th century, Bjørn’s role centered on stewardship of Holter’s holdings and the cohesion of an extended kin group whose influence stretched acros
1 CONC s Nes.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Around 1244, Bjørn married Ragnhild Audunsdatter Borg, linking the Kamp family with another established lineage. Their household became one of the most productive in the region—twelve children born across nearly three decades, each carrying th
1 CONC e Kamp name into surrounding parishes. Several sons remained tied to Holter, while others appear in later traditions as founders of collateral branches. One son, Ulfhedin Bjørnsson Kamp, is remembered with the elevated title “Baron,” marking t
1 CONC he rise of a baronial thread within the family’s later identity.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Bjørn lived his entire life at Holter, overseeing land, family alliances, and the generational continuity that defined noble standing in rural Akershus. He died there in 1300, closing an 80‑year life that anchored the Kamp family’s medieval pr
1 CONC esence. Though no written sources survive for his personal deeds, the scale of his household and the endurance of his descendants testify to his role as a stabilizing patriarch whose legacy shaped the Kamp line for centuries.
