0 @NFS2586@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Erlend Filippusson. He was a councilor of the kingdom, a judge in Gulating, the king's finance minister and for a time governor of Nordfjord. When the Norwegian council of ministers met with King Håkon VI in Kalundborg in 1376 to negotiate with th
1 CONC e Hanseatic League about their privileges in Norway, Erlend Filippusson was also present. He was married to a sister of Otto Svaleson Rømer, who was lord of the castle at Bergenhus. Erlend was in high favor with Queen Margrethe and King Håkon Mag
1 CONC nusson, but "Icelandic Annals" also praise him highly and call him "Norway's strangest man". Among other things, the Icelanders appreciated the fact that the self-confident and conservative Erlend refused to accept the title of knight, which the ki
1 CONC ng offered him, and which at that time was considered to be a particularly fine noble title. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT What an immensely high position Erlend had in our country around the year 1400 is perhaps best seen from the fact that his daughter Sigrid was married to Håkon Sigurdson of Giske, Bjarkøy and Slinde, who was, among other things, a grandson of Kin
1 CONC g Håkon Magnusson the Elder and has been called "the most well-born man in all families in Norway". In 1388-89, his family, at Queen Margreta's request, had to renounce their right to the throne of Norway so that the queen's adopted son, Erik of P
1 CONC omerania, could be elected king of Norway. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT When Queen Margrethe later, when Sigrid had become the widow of Håkon Sigurdson, as part of her amalgamation policy, had Sigrid marry the Swedish nobleman Magnus Magnusson, this may be seen as a sign that Queen Margrethe also considered Sigrid Erl
1 CONC endsdotter to be a typical representative of a high Norwegian family. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Even higher in rank and wealth than his daughter Sigrid rose Erlend's son Eindride, who has been called "Norway's most significant and influential 15th century" man at the beginning of it. He was knighted in 1397 and immediately after a member of t
1 CONC he royal council, lord of Tunsberghus from 1405 until his death in 1438, of Akershus from 1400 to 1408, "King's guardian" in Bergen in 1427, governor of Agder and Ryfylke from 1417 to 1435. Furthermore, he was the King's Norwegian courtier and a ma
1 CONC jor private shipowner. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT It was naturally also Eindride Erlendson's lot to take part in all major foreign policy negotiations and decisions that occurred at that time. Among such are the negotiations with Scotland in 1431 about the South Seas and the Isle of Man, the treat
1 CONC y with England in 1432 and the negotiations with the Swedes in 1434 and 1435. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Eindride Erlendson had a son named Erlend Eindrideson, who rose to such a high position that the royal council even proposed him for the throne under Erik of Pomerania. Furthermore, his grandson Erlend Eindrideson had a daughter, Ingegerd Erlendsdo
1 CONC tter, who was so rich in large estates and other property in many different places both in and outside our country that her wealth attracted great attention both in Sweden and in Denmark. Ingegerd Erlendsdotter had first been married to the knigh
1 CONC t Olav Guttormson at Hananger, Lista. Later she was married to the squire and royal councilor Arild Kane, whom the farmers at Sunmøre killed in 1496. When she died in 1526, she was a childless widow. That she held on to the Losna coat of arms unti
1 CONC l the last despite the fact that she had been married twice and both times to noblemen of other dre ætter, it clearly shows a precious chalice that she gave to Ulstein church in Romsdalen after the death of her last husband, for this chalice, whic
1 CONC h is adorned with beautiful images of St. Olav, St. Sunniva, St. Barbara and Maria Magdalena, is also provided with her Losna coat of arms in fine execution. According to a judgment, handed down in Oslo on 4/8. 1529, all her estates went to her nie
1 CONC ce's grandson, Mr. Erik Rosenkrantz at Bergenhus, who is known for his excellent participation in the Seven Years' War, for the Rosenkrantz Tower in Bergen, which he built and last but not least, because it was he who finally succeeded in breakin
1 CONC g the power of the Hanseatic League in Norway. As one of these will have seen, Erlend Filippusson's branch of the family reached an immeasurably high position both in social terms and in terms of wealth. That Erlend died in 1407 is stated in the Di
1 CONC plomatarium Norvegicum.
1 CONT 
1 CONT "Ei Grein av Losan 700år," Family History pamphlet, (https://www.nb.no/items/2fe52ae58fdf5e3be52107232b226ac1  need Norway IP to view), by Mikal Slevdal 1977, p.3-6
