0 @NFS2702@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Lady Sigrid Gregoriusdatter entered the world around 1246, born into a noble Norwegian family whose roots stretched across Ryfylke and the wider Stavanger region. As the daughter of Lord Baron Gregorius Andressen Pott and Princess Cicilia Haakonsda
1 CONC tter, she grew up within a lineage tied to both aristocratic authority and royal bloodlines, a heritage that shaped her place in the political and social landscape of 13th‑century Norway.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In about 1260, Sigrid married Baron Gaute Erlingssen of Talgje, forming an alliance between two influential families of Ryfylke. Their union produced four children—Hakon, Oyvor, Isak, and Cesilia—each continuing the family’s presence in the n
1 CONC oble networks of western Norway. Life on Talgje would have placed Sigrid at the center of estate management, kinship obligations, and the rhythms of medieval coastal society.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Her life came to a close in 1288, likely on Talgje, where she had spent her married years. Though the surviving records are sparse, professional research preserved in the Knaphus family collections emphasizes her role within a well‑documented nob
1 CONC le lineage. Sigrid’s memory endures today through genealogical reconstructions that highlight her position in Norway’s medieval aristocracy and her connection to families whose influence shaped the region for generations.
0 @NFS2703@ NOTE !Research by Kari Nakling; AETT og Heim 1967, p. 155; Lund Bygdebok (Historie
1 CONT I) p. 258.
