0 @NFS2720@ NOTE False Margaret (or Margareth or Margareta) (c. 1260 – 1301) was a Norwegian woman who impersonated Margaret, Maid of Norway.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The real Margaret had died in 1290 at Orkney, and her father, King Eric II, died in 1299, succeeded by his brother King Haakon V. The following year a woman arrived at Bergen, Norway in a ship from Lübeck, Germany, claiming to be Margaret, and acc
1 CONC used several people of treason. She claimed that she had not died in Orkney, had been sold by Tore Haakonsson's wife Ingeborg, and sent to Germany, where she had married. The people of Bergen and some of the clergy there supported her claim, even t
1 CONC hough the late King Erik had identified his dead daughter's body, and even though the woman appeared to be about 40 years old, whereas the real Margaret would have been 17.
1 CONT 
1 CONT She was burned at the stake for treason at Nordnes in Bergen in 1301, and her husband was beheaded.[1] She may have been used by Audun Hugleiksson as a pawn in the plot against the Maid's uncle, Haakon V.[2]
1 CONT 
1 CONT The story of the betrayed Princess was spread through popular ballads in Norway and Scotland.[3] Some years later a small St Margaret Church (Margaretaskirk) was built in Bergen near the place of execution, although this was frowned on by the autho
1 CONC rities, and it became the centre of a local martyr cult. The eventual fate of the church is uncertain, but it was probably demolished around the time of the Reformation.
0 @NFS2721@ NOTE Margaret 'the Maid of Norway, born within a year previous to 9 April 1283: acknowledged heir to the Scottish throne by the magnates of Scotland in Parliament at Scone on 5 February 1283-4, succeeded her grandfather Alexander III; was betrot
1 CONC hed to Edward, Prince of England, 6 November 1289; left Norway for Scotland in the Autumn of 1290, and died at Orkney about 26 September 1290. A 'False Margaret' who claimed to be the princess was burned at Nordness, Bergen in 1301.
