0 @NFS2920@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bese
1 CONT 
1 CONT The first member of the Bese family documented in historical sources was Jönis Bese, mentioned in 1397-1413. In the first-mentioned year, he was the knight Sten Bosson's valet and is later mentioned as district chief in Östergötland. In his seal
1 CONC , Jönis Bese carried a slanting beam coated with two arrowheads. Jönis Bese was married to Cecilia Jönsdotter, a daughter of Jönis Djäkn, Bo Jonsson's bailiff at Bjärkaholm and later at Gripsholm . Through this marriage, the family came int
1 CONC o possession of Herrsäter in Värna parish in Östergötland . which then became the main seat of the family. He and his wife's tombstone in Vadstena monasteryon the other hand, shows a shield containing a spar and below it two arrowheads and ha
1 CONC s the inscription: Sub hac petra teguntur corpora Johannis Bise et vxoris sue Cecilie Jönissadottir. Orate pro eis . In 1413, they had given the farm Hårketorp in Risinge parish to Vadstena monastery . [ 2 ]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jönis Bese had two daughters, Gunhild and Anna, married first to Per Olsson, and later to Nils Nilsson, whose son Nils called himself Bese and later formed the younger Beseätten.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jönis Bese's son was the well-born squire Sten Jönsson, who is mentioned in 1425-1458 and died in 1466. He was also in the service of a lord. Namely, in 1442 he is called Mr. Bengt Stensson's official at Göksholm , and in 1456 he serves as the k
1 CONC night Bo Stensson 's lieutenant commander in Bankekind's district in Östergötland.
0 @NFS2921@ NOTE Birger
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birger's memorial portrait at St. Bendt's Church, Ringsted
1 CONT King of Sweden
1 CONT Reign	18 December 1290 – March/April 1318
1 CONT Predecessor	Magnus III
1 CONT Successor	Magnus IV
1 CONT Born	1280
1 CONT Died	31 May 1321 (aged 40–41)
1 CONT Burial	Ringsted, Zealand
1 CONT Consort	Martha of Denmark
1 CONT House	House of Bjelbo
1 CONT Father	Magnus III of Sweden
1 CONT Mother	Hedwig of Holstein
1 CONT Religion	Roman Catholicism
1 CONT Birger[1] (Swedish: Birger Magnusson; 1280 – 31 May 1321) was King of Sweden from 1290 to 1318.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Background[edit]
1 CONT Birger was the son of King Magnus III of Sweden and Hedwig of Holstein. He was hailed king of Sweden when he was four years old. This was done by his father in order to secure the succession. In 1275, King Magnus had led a rebellion against his eld
1 CONC er brother, King Valdemar, and ousted him from the throne. Before his death, King Magnus ordered his kinsman, Torkel Knutsson, the Constable of the Realm, to be the guardian of his son Birger. In 1302, Birger was crowned at Söderköping after marr
1 CONC ying Martha of Denmark, the daughter of King Eric V of Denmark.[2]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Reign[edit]
1 CONT Birger was only ten years old when his father died, at which time Torkel Knutsson was the most influential statesman in Sweden. In 1293, Torkel Knutsson led the Swedes to a victory which won a part of western Karelia. This expedition has traditiona
1 CONC lly been dubbed as the Third Swedish Crusade. When Torkel Knutsson returned from leading the crusade in Finland, a feud had developed between the brothers. Torkel Knutsson supported King Birger.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birger came of age when there was a conflict within the Church of Sweden over interpretation of the Privileges of 1280, which had been the cost of the support of the Church for his father's usurpation. The king's brothers Erik Magnusson, Duke of S
1 CONC ödermanland and Valdemar Magnusson, Duke of Finland took advantage of this conflict. Duke Eric tried to establish an independent kingdom around Bohuslän, which he had received as part of his marriage to the Norwegian princess Ingeborg, and Hallan
1 CONC d at the boundary between Sweden, Norway and Denmark. A civil war broke out, but by 1306 emotions had cooled to the point where the dukes acknowledged the son of Birger, Magnus Birgersson, as the successor to the throne. Torkel Knutsson, who was Du
1 CONC ke Valdemar's father-in-law, was executed in 1306 as a token of reconciliation between King Birger and his brothers. The same year, in an event known as the Håtuna games (Håtunaleken), Birger was taken captive by his brothers on the Håtuna roya
1 CONC l estate in Uppland and taken as prisoner to Nyköping Castle (Nyköpingshus).[3]
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Detail of Birger's and Martha's gravestone at Ringsted
1 CONT In 1308, Eric and Valdemar were forced by the Danish king to release King Birger, but they did so under humiliating conditions. When King Birger was free, he sought aid in Denmark, and the strife began anew. Birger remained king in name, but had t
1 CONC o give up the Royal Domain, exchanging it for eastern Uppland, Närke, his brother Erik's former Duchy Södermanland, Östergötland, Gotland and the Castle of Viborg.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1312, Duke Eric married Ingeborg of Norway, daughter of King Haakon V of Norway in a double wedding in Oslo. At the same time, Eric's brother Duke Valdemar married Ingeborg Eriksdottir of Norway, the daughter of King Eric II of Norway.[4]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Duke Erik also held Bohuslän from Norway as well as northern Halland and was creating a separate kingdom centered on Göta älv. In 1317 however, Birger captured his brothers during the Nyköping Banquet (Nyköpings gästabud), which led to thei
1 CONC r death. According to Eric's Chronicle (Erikskrönikan), the dukes were starved to death in a cellar of Nyköping Castle.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birger was ousted by his brothers' supporters in 1318 and eventually went into exile under his brother-in-law King Eric VI of Denmark, taking the Royal Archives with him. His 18-year-old son Magnus had arrived that year with Danish troops to save B
1 CONC irger but an army under Canute Porse defeated them at Söderköping, practically putting an end to the conflict. Birger first fled to Gotland and left Magnus to defend the important Stegeborg Castle. Magnus was captured there only to be executed i
1 CONC n Stockholm in 1320,[5] with a younger brother Eric having died the year before and ex-king Birger dying in Denmark the year after. In 1319, the three-year-old son of Duke Erik, King Magnus VII of Norway, had then already been hailed King of Swede
1 CONC n after the short regency of his mother Duchess Ingeborg.[6]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children[edit]
1 CONT Magnus Birgersson (1300–1320)
1 CONT Eric Birgersson
1 CONT Agnes Birgersdotter
1 CONT Katarina Birgersdotter
0 @NFS2922@ NOTE BIRGER Magnusson (1280-[Sjælland] 31 May 1321, bur Ringsted Church).  He succeeded his father in 1290 as BIRGER King of Sweden.  The Icelandic Annals record that "Haqvinus…rex Norvegiæ" and "Birgerus rex Sveciæ, dux Ericus frater huius
1 CONC " signed a peace agreement "ad ostium Solbergæ prope Gothalbim" in 1302[234].  Deposed 1319.  The burial records of Ringsted record "Birgerus rex Suetie" who died "pridie Kal Jun" in 1321 and "uxor sua Margareta filia regis Erici et Agnetis" who d
1 CONC ied "VI Non Mar" in 1341[235].  m (Papal dispensation 23 Dec 1284, Stockholm 25 Nov 1298) MARGRETE of Denmark, daughter of ERIK V "Klipping" King of Denmark & his wife Agnes von Brandenburg (-2 Mar 1341, bur Ringsted Church).  Pope Martin IV issue
1 CONC d a dispensation for the marriage of "Birgero, filio Magni Sveciæ regis" and "Margaretæ filiæ Erici regis Daniæ" for 4o consanguinity dated 23 Dec 1284[236].  The Annales Lubicenses refer to the wife of "Birgerum regem…Sweorum" as "sororem re
1 CONC gis Danorum" when recording that her husband's brothers "Ericus et Waldemarus duces" captured her and her husband in 1305[237].  She was known as MÄRTA in Sweden.  The burial records of Ringsted record "Birgerus rex Suetie" who died "pridie Kal Ju
1 CONC n" in 1321 and "uxor sua Margareta filia regis Erici et Agnetis" who died "VI Non Mar" in 1341[238].  King Birger & his wife had six children: 
1 CONT 
1 CONT a)         MAGNUS Birgersson (Stockholm Sep 1300-murdered Stockholm 1 Jun 1320, bur Stockholm, Riddarholms Church).  He was probably chosen by his father as heir but was imprisoned by supporters of his cousin and finally executed[239].  Magnus ha
1 CONC d [one possible] illegitimate daughter: 
1 CONT 
1 CONT i)          KARIN Magnusdotter of Herrmanshult . 
1 CONT 
1 CONT b)         ERIK Birgersson (-1319).  The Chronica Archiepiscoporum Lundensium names "Birgerus rex Suecie, uxor sua Margareta…et tres filii sui Ericus, Otto et Valdemarus"[240].  Archdeacon at Uppsala 1315. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT c)         OTTO Birgersson .  The Chronica Archiepiscoporum Lundensium names "Birgerus rex Suecie, uxor sua Margareta…et tres filii sui Ericus, Otto et Valdemarus"[241]. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT d)         VALDEMAR Birgersson .  The Chronica Archiepiscoporum Lundensium names "Birgerus rex Suecie, uxor sua Margareta…et tres filii sui Ericus, Otto et Valdemarus"[242]. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT e)         AGNES Birgersdotter (-after 1344).  The Chronica Archiepiscoporum Lundensium names "Birgerus rex Suecie, uxor sua Margareta, filie sue Agnes et Katherina"[243]. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT f)          KATARINA Birgersdotter (-after 1320).  The Chronica Archiepiscoporum Lundensium names "Birgerus rex Suecie, uxor sua Margareta, filie sue Agnes et Katherina"[244].
