0 @NFS3037@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Gunnar Arnbjørnsson Bolt was born around 1215 at Holter in Nes, Akershus, into one of the quietly influential lendmann families of early medieval Norway. He grew up among siblings who carried the same strong patronymic—Arnbjørnsson and Arnbjør
1 CONC nsdatter—children of Arnbjørn Jonsen Ornes, a lendmann and syslemann whose authority shaped the region’s political and administrative life. Gunnar’s childhood would have been marked by the rhythms of estate management, local governance, an
1 CONC d the expectations placed on sons of noble households.
1 CONT 
1 CONT By the 1260s, Gunnar had established himself at Holstad in Ås, where he spent the rest of his life. His marriage to Gyda Guttormsdatter Haavarstad linked him to another landholding family, strengthening the network of alliances that sustained nobl
1 CONC e power in southeastern Norway. Together they raised at least two sons—Jon Gunnarsen Saltkarl and Tjostolv Gunnarson Fåle Bolt—both of whom carried forward the Bolt name and held positions tied to estates such as Haavardstad and Tomb. Their ca
1 CONC reers reflect the family’s continued involvement in regional administration and landholding responsibilities.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Gunnar’s life unfolded during a period of shifting royal authority and evolving noble structures, yet his profile suggests a man rooted in the practical duties of estate leadership rather than the turbulence of national politics. He lived to an a
1 CONC dvanced age for the era, dying in 1299 at Holstad. Though no contemporary sources survive to describe his personality or deeds, the pattern of his family’s roles—syslemenn, estate holders, and regional officials—places him firmly within the f
1 CONC abric of Norway’s medieval administrative nobility.
1 CONT 
1 CONT His legacy endures through the Bolt line and through the estates associated with his descendants, marking Gunnar as a steady link in a long chain of landholding and governing families whose quiet work shaped local life in medieval Akershus.
