0 @NFS3062@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Born around 1210 in Stårheim, Nordfjord, Gregorius Andressen Pott emerged during a formative period in Norwegian medieval governance. His early life is not documented in detail, but by the late 1210s he had risen into the ranks of the regional nob
1 CONC ility, holding the title Baron/Lendmann of Stårheim, a position that placed him among the king’s trusted local administrators. As a ridder og sysselmann, he served both as a knight and as a royal steward—roles that combined military responsibi
1 CONC lity with civil oversight in the Nordfjord region.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Gregorius married Princess Cicilia Haakonsdatter, daughter of King Håkon IV’s line, strengthening his family’s political standing and linking the Pott lineage to the royal house. Together they had two children: Andres Gregoriussøn, who contin
1 CONC ued the family’s presence at Stårheim, and Lady Sigrid Gregoriusdatter, remembered in later records tied to the noble circles of the region.
1 CONT 
1 CONT His life unfolded during a time of consolidation in Norway’s monarchy, and his service as a lendmann reflects the trust placed in him to maintain order and royal interests in western Norway. Gregorius died on 3 January 1246 in Eid, and was burie
1 CONC d in Bergen, the political and ecclesiastical center of the kingdom. His line continued through his children, forming part of the broader noble networks documented in medieval genealogical traditions.
