0 @NFS3086@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Elsebe is the only daughter of Magnus and Sofie. She was abducted by Bertil Lauritsen Hørby. The mother Sofie complained to the men's day in Stavanger in 1607 that Hørby had seduced and left "her daughter, her family and friends to the greatest c
1 CONC ontempt and distress". The judge in on/in her case may be confused and mixed with Anders Matsson Ænes ? The Judge was also named Anders Matsson. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Elsebe and her 2 legitimate brothers filed a lawsuit against her mothers mother, Gyrid's second spouses, descendants in Bergen. This happened in both 1616 and 1617. At that time she was still unmarried and represented herself and the 2 brothers. I
1 CONC f she married Anders Ænes it would be ca 1618 and Heine had to be born no earlier than 1618 if this is to be consistent. Elsebe must havedied before the smallpox tax i 1645, because then Anna Christensdatter is married to Anders Matsson Ænes.
1 CONT 
1 CONT It is not certain that she had any children with Bertil Larsen Hørby. There is also nothing to prove that she married Anders Matsson Ænes. In that case, the Ænes people would probably have named her father Mogens Heinesen, and Sofia Gyntersberg
1 CONC . 
1 CONT 
1 CONT In kvinnherad there was a merchant who also bore the name Heine. A first name does not prove any kinship. Could it be him, who is the origin of Heine in Øyre's name ?
1 CONT 
1 CONT Heine Andersen had a son who married Anders Matsson's daughter. If this daughter were to be Heine's half-sister, then this marriage stinks of incest. If Elsebe was  Heine's mother. Why is there no naming of Elsebe, Mogens, and Sofia?
1 CONT 
1 CONT Elsebe's brothers were Hans and an unnamed brother. His name might have been Mogens and identical to the Mogens/Magnus who lived for a while (1604) at Mel? Probably both brothers lived for a while at Mel, and Hans may have had a daughter there, wh
1 CONC o married Jens (Olavsen) Mel.
0 @NFS3087@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Håvard Ketilsson Galle emerges from the 13th‑century Norwegian landscape as a figure anchored firmly within the Galle family line—one of several interlinked rural noble or lendmann‑level families that appear in medieval genealogical traditio
1 CONC n. Born around 1230, likely somewhere in Norway’s southeastern districts, he was the son of Kjetil Håvardsson Galle and Eriksdatter Galle, part of a household that produced several children who carried the Galle name into the next century.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Although no direct records survive for his life—FamilySearch shows no attached sources, and the page reflects later genealogical reconstruction—his placement within the family suggests he lived during a period of shifting local authority, whe
1 CONC n landholding families balanced agricultural leadership with obligations to regional magnates. His adulthood would have unfolded during the reigns of kings Håkon Håkonsson and Magnus Lagabøte, an era marked by legal reform and consolidation of r
1 CONC oyal power.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Håvard is credited with at least one known child, Rolv Håvardsson Galle, born around 1245, indicating that Håvard reached maturity and established his own household by the mid‑13th century. The absence of a named spouse and the lack of documen
1 CONC ted events—no christening, marriage, or burial entries—reflect the fragmentary nature of rural medieval Norwegian records rather than a lack of significance.
1 CONT 
1 CONT He died sometime after mid‑century, remembered primarily through the genealogical threads preserved by descendants and later compilers. His legacy survives through the continuation of the Galle name into the 14th century, where members of the ext
1 CONC ended family appear in scattered local histories.
