0 @NFS3243@ NOTE "Pedigree Resource File," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/2:2:STMV-FFX : accessed 20 March 2018), entry for Torkell Vikingsson /Avaldsnes/; file (2:2:2:MMQ5-NY5), submitted 12 May 2011.
0 @NFS3244@ NOTE "Pedigree Resource File," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/2:2:9HF9-Y45 : accessed 20 March 2018), entry for Priest Torkell Vikingsson Avaldsnes; file (2:2:2:MM9R-QJ7), submitted 11 June 2002 by khandeland2769979
0 @NFS3245@ NOTE based on article by Olav Harstad (see Finn Torgeirsen: Historical Information)
1 CONT 
1 CONT "Erik [Sigurdsen Galtung]probably also had a daughter Olov who married Viking Torgilsson from Nordbø on Rennesøy. Olov is mentioned here 1368-1379. Much can be said to indicate that Viking and Olov moved to Aske, and that they lived here until th
1 CONC eir deaths. Viking and Olov had 2 daughters. One daughter was married to Finn Torgersen from Aga in Hardanger, who in turn had a son Viking Finnsen. The other daughter, Jorunn, was married to a Bergulf Arnesen. He is of unknown origin, but probabl
1 CONC y with some connection to Kvinesdal .
1 CONT 
1 CONT "That Olov is a daughter of Erik Galtung is not found directly in the sources. The strongest indication that she is is found in the fact that one of Torgils Vikingson on Aga's descendants, i.e. Laurits Johannesen Torsnes' son Laurits Lauritsen too
1 CONC k up the family name Galtung again , and in 1640 had his nobility approved. The Galtungs used a wild boar in their coat of arms. It must then have been Viking Finnson or his unknown wife who was descended from the Galtungs. The most likely relation
1 CONC ship is through his grandmother Olov Eriksdatter. It is somewhat striking that the knight Gaute Eriksen never mentions a sister Olov, but in a letter from 1412, [1] where Gaute Eriksen draws up a will for his sister's children, i.e. the children o
1 CONC f Guri and Gunnar Kane, it is said as follows: "......first Testament, against the promise that they and Mr. Gautes other heirs promised to fulfill his later Testament". It can be interpreted that Guri had a sister, and her inheritance is set up i
1 CONC n another will.
1 CONT 
1 CONT "Viking Torgilsson Nordbø is mentioned in 1379, ref. DN b. 4 letter 513. Two men then announce that Viking Thorgilson sold with his wife's consent to Thorkel Thorlakson 3 Månedsmatsbol (mmb.) [2] in Skeie in Hundvåg. Based on the letter, it seem
1 CONC s that it was the wife Olov who owned the farm Skeie. Viking is also mentioned in 1368, then as a bailiff. [3]
1 CONT 
1 CONT "As mentioned, Viking and Olov had 2 daughters who were married to their sons-in-law, Finn Torgersen and Bergulf Arnesen respectively. In a letter of 1423 [4] , Gard Toreson, magistrate in Ryfylke and Guttorm Eivindson, governor of Lista announc
1 CONC e a settlement between the two sons-in-law Finn Torgeirson and Bergulf Arneson about Aske on Mosterøy. We note that Bergulv Arnesson acts on behalf of his wife Jorunn. The settlement probably came about after Finn's wife had died, and the knight E
1 CONC indrid Erlendssen, who was governor of Ryfylke , acts on Finn's behalf. It appears from the letter that these two each own half of the farm Aske, and that the share in the farm Vaule has been divided in the same way between them. From the magistrat
1 CONC es we know that Gard Toreson lived at Talgje and that Finn Torgeirson probably came from there, so it is quite likely that there was a connection here. It must also be noted that the governor of Lista was supposed to come to Finnøy to help arrang
1 CONC e the settlement. It is unlikely that this would have been done if he had not had close contact with the parties."
