0 @NFS3853@ NOTE https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Porse#Tab_8
0 @NFS3854@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT 🌿 The Owners of Straume: A Legacy of Faith and Family
1 CONT 
1 CONT In the early 1500s, the farm at Straume was held by a man named Jens Findson. He was likely a landowner of some standing, for records show that he sold the property to Erik Walckendorf, the Archbishop of Nidaros, sometime between 1510 and 1522. Wit
1 CONC h that sale, Straume became part of the holdings of the archbishop’s estate, and was leased out for an annual rent of four øyrar—a modest but meaningful sum in those days.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Yet by around 1530, Straume disappears from the church’s land records. It’s possible that the archbishop traded it away or sold it, perhaps in exchange for other farmland. Not long after, the farm came into the hands of Gyri Jensdotter and he
1 CONC r husband Mikkel. Their names mark a turning point in the story—not just of ownership, but of legacy.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 📜 A Testament of Truth: The Gift of Land and Legacy
1 CONT 
1 CONT On a summer day—July 4th, 1577, in Sykkylven—a group of respected men gathered to bear witness to a matter of family, faith, and inheritance. Among them were Peder Olufsen of Riksheim, Olle Pedersen the lensmann, Jørgen Pedersen, Lauritz Eriks
1 CONC en of Straumsheim, Povel, Anders, Helge of Strømme, and Olle—all men of standing in the community. Together, they signed an open letter, affirming what they had heard and known to be true.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The widow Berette Jakobsdatter, once married to Thorkjell Sjursen of Hevne, had asked these men to confirm a story passed down through the family: that Thorkjell’s beloved grandmother, Gyri Jensdotter, had gifted him two farms—Straume and Ramst
1 CONC ad—in the presence of her children, gathered in Bergen.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 🌾 A Grandmother’s Devotion - Gyri Jensdatter
1 CONT 
1 CONT According to Peder Olsen, Gyri had given the land freely and with a full heart, supported by the unanimous agreement of her children. Her intention was clear and noble: she wanted her grandson Thorkjell Sjursen, son of Anne Michelsdatter, to use th
1 CONC e land “for the advancement of God’s word,” so that he could support himself through schooling. It was a gift not just of property, but of purpose.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Gyri’s wish was that no other of Thorkjell’s siblings—not Even Sjursen, Gulbrand Sjursen, nor Gjertrud Sjursdatter—should claim the land. It was meant solely for Thorkjell, to be his forever, a legacy of love and learning.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 💍 A Promise Sealed
1 CONT 
1 CONT Peder Olsen swore to the truth of this account, pledging both his soul and his honor. If further proof was ever needed, he promised to stand again with more witnesses and more testimony. To seal their words, the men placed their marks and seals upo
1 CONC n the letter, a lasting record of Gyri’s gift and the community’s affirmation.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 🌿 Gyri’s Wish Fulfilled: A Legacy of Faith, Land, and Lineage
1 CONT 
1 CONT It seems that Gyri Jonsdotter’s heartfelt wish—to see her grandson thrive through education and faith—was beautifully fulfilled. Her grandson, Torkjell Sjursen, did indeed become a priest, receiving a calling in Hemne, a parish nestled in wha
1 CONC t is now Sør-Trøndelag, though at the time it belonged to Nordmøre.
1 CONT Sykkylven Bygdebok #2, page 296-297
1 CONT 🌾 Gyri’s Gift: A Grandmother’s Legacy of Faith and Learning
1 CONT 
1 CONT In the mid-1500s, a woman named Gyri Jonsdatter, married to Mikkel, stood as matriarch over the farms of Straume and Straumgjerde. She was no ordinary landholder—Gyri was a woman of conviction and vision. Around 1550, gathered in Bergen with he
1 CONC r children, she made a profound decision: she gifted both Straume and Ramstad to her grandson, Torkjell Sjursen, not for wealth or status, but “for the promotion of God’s word.” Her wish was that he use the land to pursue schooling and serv
1 CONC e a higher calling.
1 CONT 
1 CONT This act of devotion was later confirmed in a legal testimony by Berette Jakobsdatter, Torkjell’s widow, in Sykkylven on July 4, 1577. The letter, signed and sealed by respected men of the region—including Peder Oluffssen of Riksheim, Olle Pede
1 CONC rssen, and others—affirmed that Gyri, with her children’s blessing, had given the farms to her grandson with the express condition that they remain in his hands alone, not to be divided among other relatives. It was a sacred trust, one that Ber
1 CONC ette and the witnesses swore to uphold “both in heaven and on earth.”
1 CONT 
1 CONT 📜 From Archbishop to Heir: The Shifting Stewardship of Straume
1 CONT 
1 CONT Before Gyri’s time, Straume had passed through ecclesiastical hands. In the early 1500s, Jens Findson sold the farm to Erik Walckendorf, the Archbishop of Nidaros. For a time, the land was leased out for an annual rent, but by the 1530s, it had v
1 CONC anished from church records—likely sold or exchanged. Not long after, Gyri and Mikkel emerged as the new stewards, and with Gyri’s gift to Torkjell, the land began a new chapter rooted in faith and education.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 🙏 A Calling Fulfilled: Torkjell the Priest of Hemne
1 CONT 
1 CONT Gyri’s hopes were not in vain. Her grandson Torkjell Sjursen answered the call and became a priest in Hemne, then part of Nordmøre. He and Berette had a son, Sjur Torkjellson, who settled at Helgebostad on Hitra. Sjur inherited not only Straum
1 CONC e and Ramstad but also the spiritual and familial legacy entrusted to him. He had many children, and among them were daughters Adelus, Lisbet, and Bergitte, each of whom inherited substantial land.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 🛖 Lisbet Steinsdotter and the Women Who Carried the Line
1 CONT 
1 CONT One of the most intriguing figures to emerge from this lineage is Lisbet Steinsdotter. She first married Rasmus, believed to be another son of Sjur. By 1629/30, Lisbet had declared ownership of 16 mællag of land—a sign of her prosperity and inde
1 CONC pendence. Among her holdings was Straume with Straumgjerde, which she later brought into her second marriage with Hallstein Olsen Gjerde. Around 1630, they settled in Straumgjerde, and in 1648, Hallstein sold the property to Christopher Pedersen Hi
1 CONC erman, the priest of Borgund.
1 CONT 
1 CONT By 1661, Hierman still held the majority of the estate, though Sykkylven Church had reclaimed a portion. Around 1670, Hierman sold Straume to Lars Olsen Abelset, who was married to none other than Lisbet Steinsdotter herself—bringing the land ful
1 CONC l circle back into the family.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 🕊️ A Journey to Ytterøya: Faith, Land, and Loss
1 CONT 
1 CONT On March 15, 1563, we find Torkild Sjurdson, the priest of Hemne, visiting Ytterøya. He stayed with Mr. Staffen, the local parish lord, to settle a matter of inheritance. His wife, Berit Jakobsdatter, had inherited 1 spann of land in Rennan, and n
1 CONC ow wished to sell it to Arne Kåreson of Gildberg, who held the closest right of preemption. Acting on behalf of Berit and their late son Olav, Torkild finalized the sale, witnessed by Staffen and two local farmers.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Berit was likely the daughter of Ingeborg Bessedotter, granddaughter of Besse from Sula, tying the family to coastal settlements in Sula, Titran, and Hemne. Their descendants held land in Skogn for over a century, enduring financial hardship but al
1 CONC ways striving to keep the land within the family. Øystein Olsson redeemed the spann in 1581, and Sjur Torkildsson reclaimed it again in 1600.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 🌍 The Riiber Line: From Clerk to Citizen
1 CONT 
1 CONT By 1645, a new name entered the story: Anders Nielsen Riiber, an immigrant appointed as district clerk in Fosen. Though he resigned due to the rigors of travel, he settled in Ansnes on Hitra, married Margareta, daughter of Hemne’s priest Mr. Melc
1 CONC hior, and prospered. Anders owned land in Rennan and Stor-Gjemle, likely acquired through inheritance or purchase. His holdings suggest a connection to the same lineage that once included Pål Bårdsson of Titran and Besse of Sula.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Even Arne Kåreson of Gildberg, who purchased Berit’s spann, belonged to this extended family network. The legacy of land, faith, and kinship echoed in Archbishop Arne Einarsson’s will: “We want our heirs to inherit the landed properties we h
1 CONC old on Ytterøya and throughout Skeynafylket, as we have inherited this land.”
1 CONT 
1 CONT 🏡 A Living Tapestry
1 CONT 
1 CONT From Gyri’s heartfelt gift to Torkjell, to Lisbet’s stewardship and the Riiber family’s coastal migrations, this story is more than a record of land—it’s a testament to the enduring power of family, faith, and legacy.
1 CONT Sykkylven Bygdebok #2, page 298
