0 @NFS3869@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT In addition to their daughter Margrete, they also had a son, Erlend Filippusson of Losna, who is first mentioned in 1366 and is a councilor of the kingdom as early as 1369. He was with a number of Norway's other great men in negotiations that hav
1 CONC e been decisive for the history of Norway. Among other things, he was present at the negotiations in Kalundborg in 1376. In Icelandic annals it is said of him that he was Norway's strangest man.
1 CONT 
1 CONT He was a relative of Torgeir Torsteinson (Mita ?) and was his ombudsman in the inheritance of Gaute Håkonson in Tolga. See about this under Aga-Torstein Mita. He died in 1407. A daughter of Erlend Filipusson, Sigrid Erlendsdatter, was married to H
1 CONC åkon Sigurdson of Giske, Bjarkey and Slinde in Sogn, of the powerful family of the Havtorssønnenes, who were close relatives of the old royal house. Erlend's son, Eindride Erlendsson, became a knight in 1397 and soon after a member of the Counci
1 CONC l of State. He was one of Norway's most powerful men in the early 15th century. Like his father, he was involved in many decisions that have had an impact on our history. He was ombudsman for Finn Torgeirson in 1423. Mr. Eindride Erlandson had a so
1 CONC n, Mr. Erlend Eindrideson of Losna. About Mr. We know that Erlend was a prominent man who participated a lot in the country's government.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1450 he was among 20 other councilors who elected Christian I as Norwegian king. With him the male line at Losna became extinct, but he had two daughters who later owned the estate. His daughter Ingegerd was married to Mr. Olav Guttormson of Han
1 CONC anger at Lister. This marriage was childless. After Mr. Olav's death she was married to councilor Arild Kane, whom the farmers of Sunnmøre killed in 1496. The second daughter of Mr. Erlend was married to Bo Fleming of Nesøen in Asker, and from th
1 CONC eir daughter Margrete, who married Holger Rosenkrantz of Boller, the sect later comes through marriage in connection with the Rantzau and Thott families. More about these can be found in "Danmarks Adels Aarbog".
1 CONT 
1 CONT This was a little about the main branch of the Losnaætten, which belonged to the high nobility. As we have seen, Viking Finnson, who changed his name to Aga, has connections to this branch of the Losnaætten. Cf. the Aga line.
1 CONT 
1 CONT "Slektene Velde og Espeland og inngiftede slekter," History book (https://www.nb.no/items/bfd60befe5728733c5f9130b93ca7ce7 need Norway IP to see), by Berge Velde, 1957. p.240
