0 @NFS3914@ NOTE 🌿 The Owners of Straume: A Legacy of Faith and Family
1 CONT 
1 CONT In the early 1500s, the farm at Straume was held by a man named Jens Findson. He was likely a landowner of some standing, for records show that he sold the property to Erik Walckendorf, the Archbishop of Nidaros, sometime between 1510 and 1522. Wit
1 CONC h that sale, Straume became part of the holdings of the archbishop’s estate, and was leased out for an annual rent of four øyrar—a modest but meaningful sum in those days.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Yet by around 1530, Straume disappears from the church’s land records. It’s possible that the archbishop traded it away or sold it, perhaps in exchange for other farmland. Not long after, the farm came into the hands of Gyri Jonsdotter and he
1 CONC r husband Mikkel. Their names mark a turning point in the story—not just of ownership, but of legacy.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 📜 A Testament of Truth: The Gift of Land and Legacy
1 CONT 
1 CONT On a summer day—July 4th, 1577, in Sykkylven—a group of respected men gathered to bear witness to a matter of family, faith, and inheritance. Among them were Peder Olufsen of Riksheim, Olle Pedersen the lensmann, Jørgen Pedersen, Lauritz Eriks
1 CONC en of Straumsheim, Povel, Anders, Helge of Strømme, and Olle—all men of standing in the community. Together, they signed an open letter, affirming what they had heard and known to be true.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The widow Berette Jakobsdatter, once married to Thorkjell Sjursen of Hevne, had asked these men to confirm a story passed down through the family: that Thorkjell’s beloved grandmother, Gyri Jonsdotter, had gifted him two farms—Straume and Ramst
1 CONC ad—in the presence of her children, gathered in Bergen.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 🌾 A Grandmother’s Devotion
1 CONT 
1 CONT According to Peder Olsen, Gyri had given the land freely and with a full heart, supported by the unanimous agreement of her children. Her intention was clear and noble: she wanted her grandson Thorkjell Sjursen, son of Anne Michelsdatter, to use th
1 CONC e land “for the advancement of God’s word,” so that he could support himself through schooling. It was a gift not just of property, but of purpose.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Gyri’s wish was that no other of Thorkjell’s siblings—not Even Sjursen, Gulbrand Sjursen, nor Gjertrud Sjursdatter—should claim the land. It was meant solely for Thorkjell, to be his forever, a legacy of love and learning.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 💍 A Promise Sealed
1 CONT 
1 CONT Peder Olsen swore to the truth of this account, pledging both his soul and his honor. If further proof was ever needed, he promised to stand again with more witnesses and more testimony. To seal their words, the men placed their marks and seals upo
1 CONC n the letter, a lasting record of Gyri’s gift and the community’s affirmation.
1 CONT Sykkylven Bygdebok #2, page 296
