0 @NFS4145@ NOTE Løføen
0 @NFS4146@ NOTE A document dated 31 Jan 1337 appears to describe a court case resulting from a feud (ættestriden). One party was Torleiv Saksabjørnsson and his son Bjørn, as well as Bjørn Saksabjørnsson and other people from Lindheim. The opposing par
1 CONC ty consisted of Erling Haukvik, Tord Låvell and his son Steinulv, and relatives from Haukvik. Both parties complained of murder, weapon damage and various other offenses. Torleiv was fined for killing Hallvard Ellingsson, and Bjørn Saksabjørnsso
1 CONC n was fined for striking Geirmund Erlingsson with an ax.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Torleiv was to give two merks of gold for the murder of Hallvard Erlingsson and his brother Bjørn Saksabjørnsson four merks of silver to Geirmund Erlingsson for axing. This was probably an old family dispute, and on a convention day in Skien late
1 CONC r in 1337, the parties came together and said they were willing to uphold the verdict. The documents show that the people at Lindheim were the king's handmaidens
0 @NFS4147@ NOTE Torleiv Saksabjørnsson [child of Saksabjørn Lindheim] of Lindheim and his son Bjørn are mentioned in 1337. Torleiv was married to Cecilia (Sissel) Ivarsdotter of Søndre Nes, fairly certainly the daughter of provincial judge Ivar Ogmunds
1 CONC son Rova.
1 CONT Torleiv Saksabjørnsson did not live long after the big feud in the 1330s. He probably died around 1340.
