0 @NFS4355@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Hallvard Toraldesen was born around 1395 in Nes parish, Akershus, a farming and river‑valley community in Øvre Romerike. He grew up in a household rooted in local landholding traditions, the son of Taralde Olavsen and Aasa Ulvsdatter, whose fami
1 CONC lies were part of the established agrarian network that shaped medieval Nes.
1 CONT 
1 CONT As a young man, Hallvard became associated with the Rustad farm, a name preserved in later records as his alternate identifier. Farm names in this period functioned as locational aliases, marking where a person lived, worked, or held ties. His lif
1 CONC e unfolded during a time of shifting political authority in Norway, yet rural communities like Nes remained anchored in stable patterns of land, kinship, and seasonal labor.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Hallvard formed a household with Gudrun Olavsdatter Nesøya, and together they raised at least one known child, Gyrid Hallvardsdatter Holter, born in 1414. Through Gyrid, Hallvard’s lineage continued into the next generation of Romerike families
1 CONC , linking Rustad and Holter farm networks.
1 CONT 
1 CONT He lived his entire life within the bounds of Nes parish, passing away there in 1475, having witnessed the slow transition from medieval to early‑modern Norway. His name survives in the record through patronymic variants—Toraldesen and Toraldse
1 CONC n—and through the enduring farm identity of Rustad, tying him to the land and community that shaped his world.
0 @NFS4356@ NOTE This system definitely gets confusing when you encounter names as common as Ole Olsen or Nils Hansen, so they often used their farm names to clarify their identity when conducting official business or traveling. In church records, for examp
1 CONC le, they would typically append the farm name to the patronymic name to be more precise, for example “Anders Olafsen Sandness” (or “Anders Olafsen på (from) Sandness” if Anders merely worked at Sandness). It’s very important to keep in m
1 CONC ind that when a farm name is used, it is the farm at which they currently reside, rather than the farm at which they were born. The use of farm names is very helpful to keep in mind when looking at old records, since Norway only gradually adopted f
1 CONC ixed surnames from the mid-1800s until they were required in 1923.
