0 @NFS4383@ NOTE Mechtilde af Holsten (født ca. 1220, død 1288) var en dansk dronning.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Liv og gerning:
1 CONT Mechtilde var datter af grev Adolf 4. af Holsten og blev i 1237 gift med Valdemar Sejrs søn Abel, der var hertug i Sønderjylland.[1] Herved knyttedes Abel nøje til den holstenske greveslægt og blev formynder for sine unge svogre, da grev Adol
1 CONC f gik i kloster. Da Abel blev konge, kronedes Mechtilde sammen med ham den 1. november 1250, men da Christoffer efter Abels død overtog kronen, måtte hun forlade Danmark og udholde en hård kamp for at bevare hertugdømmet i Sønderjylland for si
1 CONC n slægt. Under denne kamp sluttede hun sig til kongens værste fjende Jakob Erlandsen.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Den 12. maj 1260 pantsatte Mechtilde sammen med sine sønner Erik og Abel Abelsen landet mellem Ejderen og Slien (Stapelholm, Fræslet, Svansen og Krongodset Jernved) til sine brødre, Johan 1. af Holsten og Gerhard 1. af Holsten[2][3] samt yderlig
1 CONC ere fæstningen Rendsborg, som hun havde modtaget som medgift[4], og hendes virksomhed dannede i det hele grundlaget for Holstens stadig voksende indflydelse i Sønderjylland, hvorfor hun også var forhadt af danskerne, som Rydårbogen bevidner. E
1 CONC t led i Mechtildes politiske bestræbelser var også hendes giftermål med Sveriges rigsstyrer Birger Jarl i 1261. Efter dennes død 1266 levede Mechtilde i Holsten.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Børn:
1 CONT Valdemar 3. Abelsøn –1257; hertug i Sønderjylland
1 CONT Erik 1. ca. 1241–1272; hertug i Sønderjylland
1 CONT Sofia Abelsdatter af Danmark ca. 1240–1284
1 CONT Abel Abelssøn 1252–1279
1 CONT 
1 CONT Noter:
1 CONT 1. Albrectsen, s. 24
1 CONT 2. Albrectsen, s. 45, 217
1 CONT 3. Diplomatarium Danicum. Række 2, Bind 1: 1250–1265. Munksgaard, København 1938, Nr. 316.
1 CONT 4. Horst Windmann: Schleswig als Territorium. Grundzüge der Verfassungsentwicklung im Herzogtum Schleswig von den Anfängen bis zum Aussterben des Abelschen Hauses 1375 (= Quellen und Forschungen zur Geschichte Schleswig-Holsteins. Bd. 30, ISSN 01
1 CONC 73-0940). Wachholtz, Neumünster 1954, S. 172 (Zugleich: Kiel, Universität, Dissertation, 1952).
1 CONT 
1 CONT Litteratur:
1 CONT Esben Albrectsen: Herredømmet over Sønderjylland 1375-1404; København 1981; ISBN 87-87462-18-4
1 CONT Eksterne henvisninger
1 CONT Jørgen Olrik: "Mechtilde" i Salmonsens Konversationsleksikon, 2. udgave, bind XVI; s. 821
1 CONT Kr. Erslev: "Mechtilde" i Dansk Biografisk Leksikon, Bind XI (1897), s. 205-206
1 CONT 
1 CONT Denne side blev senest ændret den 26. september 2019 kl. 23:42.
0 @NFS4384@ NOTE Matilda of Holstein or Mechthild (1220 or 1225 – 1288 in Kiel) was a Danish queen consort, married to King Abel of Denmark and later to Birger Jarl, Regent of Sweden.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Life:
1 CONT Matilda was the daughter of Adolf IV, Count of Holstein, and Heilwig of Lippe. On 25 April 1237 she was married to Abel of Denmark in Schleswig. The marriage was arranged to form a tie between Holstein and Sønderjylland. In 1239, Abel became the g
1 CONC uardian of her minor brothers.
1 CONT 
1 CONT When Abel became king in 1250, she was crowned with him in Roskilde on 1 November. When Abel died in 1252, he was succeeded by his brother rather than her son Valdemar, who was imprisoned at Cologne at the time, and she was forced to leave Denmar
1 CONC k and enter a convent.
1 CONT 
1 CONT She managed to get her son Valdemar released from the captivity of the Archbishop of Cologne and fought for the inheritance of her children in the Duchy of Schleswig. In 1253, she secured the Duchy of Sønderjylland for her son Valdemar.[1]
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1260, her son Valdemar died, and she secured the Duchy for her next son, Erik. The same year, however, she pawned the areas Eider and Schlei in southern Denmark to her brothers.
1 CONT 
1 CONT She made a pact with Jacob Erlandsen, archbishop of Lund, and then broke her vows from the convent by marrying the Swedish regent Birger Jarl in 1261. Birger had been one of her late husband Abel's major antagonists who had started up a military ve
1 CONC ndetta against him which was only stopped by Abel's death.[2] After Birger's death in 1266, Matilda moved to Kiel, yet her own grave is with Birger's in Varnhem, Sweden.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1288, shortly before her death, she gave up Eider and Schlei to her brothers. She was unpopular in Denmark, where she was called the "daughter of the Devil"[3] and accused of destroying letters from the Pope and emperor to King Valdemar II.[4]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Issue:
1 CONT Queen Matilda bore her first husband three sons and a daughter:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Valdemar III (1238–1257); Duke of Schleswig 1254–1257
1 CONT Sophie (born 1240, date of death unknown)
1 CONT Eric I (c. 1241 – 27 May 1272); Duke of Schleswig 1260–1272
1 CONT Abel (1252–1279)
1 CONT 
1 CONT References:
1 CONT 1. Dansk Biografisk Leksikon
1 CONT 2. Dr. Ulf Sundberg in Medeltidens svenska krig ISBN 9189660110 pp. 78–79
1 CONT 3. Dansk Biografisk Leksikon
1 CONT 4. Dansk Biografisk Leksikon
1 CONT 
1 CONT This page was last edited on 29 July 2020, at 12:36 (UTC).
