0 @NFS4439@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bj%C3%B8lstad_Farm?wprov=sfla1
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Copied from Notes in this PID.
1 CONT NOTE.  2nd wife not mentioned.  See several family trees from different websites in Memories.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Øystein Guttormsen Bratt, born about 1475/75 at Bjølstad in Gudbrandsdalen, died about 1545 or just after this year.  It was not common for a farmer to get married very early at that time and in Bjølstadætta they were rather mature men when the
1 CONC y got a wife.  There is therefore no reason to believe that Øystein has been an exception.  He was probably married approx.  1505/10 with Tora Tordsdatter from Tjerne on Ringsaker, she has undoubtedly been very young then, in contrast to what wa
1 CONC s usual.  It was great care and heavy work.  It was therefore not considered that a girl was able to work and suitable to take on the board before she was well into her 20s, but Tora seems to have been different.  She and Øystei had 9 children wh
1 CONC o became adults.  Since she had so many children, she must have been married young.  She should therefore have been born around 1485/90.  She was the daughter of Tord Torgunnson and Birgitte Kjellsdotter Tjerne.  He was a storekeeper together wit
1 CONC h his father on 17 January 1518 at Bjølstad.  February 2, 1530 in Lom he and Hennin Munk (Lassesson) changed land, so Øystein got the farm "Annestad, Ånstad, on Skjåkstrondi with half the mill that lay to Skiåker", and Henning Munk got "Svadeg
1 CONC ard" on Dovre (DN lll 1122)  .  In 1534, Øystein won the case against Audulv Bøje et al.  from the court in Oppland.  The last time he is mentioned is when he was a witness on March 11, 1543 at Bjølstad, when Jon Andersson Kleppe gave his son Kn
1 CONC ut garden Mo (DN X 717).  During the diploma from 1530, his seal hung on.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Østen Guttormssøn Bratt to Bjølstad.  Østen = Øistein = Eystein.  The many farms on Hedmark and Oppland that Brattene owned, probably came with his wife Tore Torsdatter Tjerne.  When Østen Bratt died, it appears that large parts of the estat
1 CONC e were mortgaged, so that the family did not have full control over it.
