0 @NFS4512@ NOTE Holstein (German pronunciation: [ˈhɔlʃtaɪn] (Loudspeaker.svglisten); Northern Low Saxon: Holsteen; Danish: Holsten; Latin: Holsatia) is the region between the rivers Elbe and Eider. It is the southern half of Schleswig-Holstein, the nor
1 CONC thernmost state of Germany.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Holstein_Arms.svg
1 CONT Coat of arms of Holstein: a stylised nettle leaf; similar to the coat of arms of Schaumburg
1 CONT Holstein once existed as the German County of Holstein (German: Grafschaft Holstein; 811–1474), the later Duchy of Holstein (German: Herzogtum Holstein; 1474–1866), and was the northernmost territory of the Holy Roman Empire. The history of Hol
1 CONC stein is closely intertwined with the history of the Danish Duchy of Schleswig (Danish: Slesvig). The capital of Holstein is Kiel.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Holstein's name comes from the Holcetae, a Saxon tribe mentioned by Adam of Bremen as living on the north bank of the Elbe, to the west of Hamburg. The name means "dwellers in the wood" or "hill-sitters" (Northern Low Saxon: Hol(t)saten; German: Ho
1 CONC lzsassen).
1 CONT 
1 CONT Limes.saxoniae.wmt.png
1 CONT The Limes Saxoniae
1 CONT History
1 CONT 
1 CONT Origins
1 CONT After the Migration Period of the Early Middle Ages, Holstein was adjacent to the Obotrites on the coast of the Baltic Sea and the land of the Danes in Jutland.
1 CONT 
1 CONT With the conquest of Old Saxony by Charlemagne circa 800, he granted the land north of the Eider River (Schleswig) to the Danes by the Treaty of Heiligen signed in 811. The ownership of what would late become eastern Holstein (districts of Plön an
1 CONC d Ostholstein) was given to the Obotrites, namely the Wagrians, and the Saxon elite was deported to various areas of the empire. After 814, however, the Saxons were restored to Western Holstein. The Wagrians were pushed out of the Limes Saxonia
1 CONC e - the new border running from the Elbe River near Boizenburg northwards along the Bille River to the mouth of the Schwentine at the Kiel Fjord and the Baltic Sea. For the following 300 years, Holstein continued to be a part of Saxony.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The County of Holstein
1 CONT Undivided Holstein by 1250 with neighbouring states[note 1]
1 CONT Undivided Holstein by 1250 with neighbouring states
1 CONT The new county of Holstein was established in 1111; it was first a fief of the Duchy of Saxony, then of the Duchy of Saxe-Lauenburg, and finally of the Prince-Bishopric of Lübeck. With the establishment of the new territorial unit, expansion to th
1 CONC e East began and the Wagrians were finally defeated in 1138.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The County of Holstein was ruled by the House of Schaumburg; the first count was Adolf I, Count of Holstein. Holstein was temporarily occupied by Denmark after the Battle of Stellau (1201), but was reconquered by the Count of Schauenburg and his al
1 CONC lies in the Battle of Bornhöved (1227).
1 CONT 
1 CONT Partitions of the County of Holstein (1111–1474)
1 CONT The Counts of Schauenburg and Holstein partitioned Holstein several times among the inheriting sons into up to six lines, named after their towns of residence:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Holstein-Itzehoe, branch county between 1261 and 1290, partitioned from Holstein, repartitioned into Holstein-Pinneberg, Holstein-Plön and Holstein-Rendsburg
1 CONT Holstein-Kiel, branch county between 1261 and 1390, partitioned from Holstein, in 1273 Holstein-Segeberg (first) was partitioned from Holstein-Kiel, but reverted in 1308, but then lost to Holstein-Pinneberg, Holstein-Plön and Holstein-Rendsburg i
1 CONC n 1316; Holstein-Kiel acquired Holstein-Plön in 1350, and merged itself into Holstein-Rendsburg
1 CONT Holstein-Pinneberg, branch county between 1290 and 1640, partitioned from Holstein-Itzehoe, acquired a share of Holstein-Segeberg in 1316, merged into the Duchy of Holstein
1 CONT Holstein-Plön, branch county between 1290 and 1390, partitioned from Holstein-Itzehoe, acquired a share of Holstein-Segeberg in 1316, merged into Holstein-Kiel
1 CONT Holstein-Rendsburg, branch county between 1290 and 1474, partitioned from Holstein-Itzehoe, acquired a share of Holstein-Segeberg (first) in 1316, and Holstein-Kiel in 1390, in 1381/1384 Holstein-Segeberg (second) was partitioned from Holstein-Rend
1 CONC sburg, but reverted in 1403, elevated to ducal rank in 1474
1 CONT Holstein-Segeberg (first), branch county between 1273 and 1308, partitioned from and reverted to Holstein-Kiel, but seized by allied Holstein-Pinneberg, Holstein-Plön, and Holstein-Rendsburg, partitioning Segeberg in three shares, each merged int
1 CONC o one of the lines in 1316
1 CONT Holstein-Segeberg (second), branch county between 1381/1384 and 1403, partitioned from and reverted to Holstein-Rendsburg
1 CONT In 1386 King Oluf II of Denmark and his mother-regent, Queen Margaret I, enfeoffed in Nyborg Gerhard VI, Count of Holstein-Rendsburg and his cognatic successors with the Duchy of Schleswig. He thus became as Gerhard II duke of Schleswig. Until 139
1 CONC 0 the Rendsburg branch united by inheritance all branches except of that of Holstein-Pinneberg.
1 CONT 
1 CONT When the Holstein-Rendsburg line of the Schauenburg counts became extinct with the death of Adolf VIII of Holstein-Rendsburg (and in personal union as Adolf I Duke of Schleswig) in 1459, Christian I of Denmark inherited – from his maternal uncl
1 CONC e Adolf I – the Duchy of Schleswig, a Danish fief. Through the Treaty of Ribe (1460) Christian was elected Count of Holstein-Rendsburg, then still a Saxe-Lauenburgian subfief within the Holy Roman Empire.
1 CONT ...article continues with the duchy of Holstein c 1474....
0 @NFS4513@ NOTE Hedwig of Holstein, Queen consort of Sweden
1 CONT Tenure	             1276–1290
1 CONT Coronation	     29 June 1281
1 CONT Born	                     before 1264
1 CONT Died	                     c. 1325
1 CONT Burial	             Riddarholm Church
1 CONT Spouse	             Magnus III of Sweden
1 CONT Issue	             Ingeborg, Queen of Denmark
1 CONT                              Birger, King of Sweden
1 CONT                              Eric, Duke of Sudermannia
1 CONT                              Valdemar, Duke of Finland
1 CONT                              Richeza Magnusdotter
1 CONT House	             House of Schauenburg
1 CONT Father	             Gerhard I, Count of Holstein-Itzehoe
1 CONT Mother	             Elisabeth of Mecklenburg
1 CONT 
1 CONT Hedwig of Holstein[1] or Helvig[2][3][4][5][6](Swedish: Helvig,[7] German: Helwig) (1260–1324) was Queen of Sweden as the consort of King Magnus III Barnlock. Her parents were Gerhard I, Count of Holstein-Itzehoe (died 1290) and Elisabeth of Meck
1 CONC lenburg (died 1280).
1 CONT 
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT Hedwig was married to Magnus Ladulås in 1276, and was granted the fief of Dåvö in Munktorp in Västmanland. Magnus succeeded in preventing a Danish-Holstein alliance by marrying her. However, he had obtained a dispensation for their marriage onl
1 CONC y after the wedding.[8]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Her father was captured during the Folkunge party revolt (Folkungaupproret) by rebellious noblemen in Skara in 1278 and the queen was also targeted. The actions of the rebels were well timed; they coincided with her journey through Sweden. She soug
1 CONC ht refuge in the convent in the city.[9]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Hedwig was crowned Queen of Sweden in the city of Söderköping on 29 June 1281; this is the first confirmed coronation of a queen consort in Sweden. It included the prayers for her fertility, which was the matter of great importance.[10] She found
1 CONC ed the Greyfriars convent (Gråmunkekloster) in Stockholm and several other churches and convents. As a queen, however, she is not very much heard of, despite the fact that she held the position for fourteen years, she lived a discreet life, both a
1 CONC s a queen and as a dowager queen. She took a prominent part in processions which accompanied the inauguration of bishops, celebrations of a feast day and the installation of relics, such as the Mass for Saint Erik in 1277.[11]
1 CONT 
1 CONT After the death of her spouse in 1290, Hedwig acted as one of the executors of the will of the King. In 1291, she withdrew to her estate Dåvö in Västmanland. She is not known to have taken any political role, formal or informal, during or afte
1 CONC r the reign of her spouse. She was described as a noble, loyal and peace-loving mother figure, tormented by the conflicts between her sons. She acted as a foster mother for her son's future bride, Martha of Denmark, who spent a lot of her childhoo
1 CONC d in Sweden as the future Queen of Sweden after 1290. In 1302, she was present at the coronation of her son.
1 CONT 
1 CONT As dowager queen, she governed Fjärdhundraland, which was given to her as dower.[12]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Queen Hedwig is buried in Riddarholm Church in Stockholm, with her husband and her daughter Richeza.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children
1 CONT Hedwig's wedding took place at Kalmar castle on 11 November 1276 with king Magnus III Ladulås of Sweden. They had the following children:[13]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Ingeborg Magnusdotter of Sweden, born abt. 1279. Married King Eric VI of Denmark, Erik Menved.
1 CONT Birger Magnusson, born abt. 1280, king of Sweden.
1 CONT Eric Magnusson, Duke of Södermanland in 1302 and Halland etc. c 1305, born abt. 1282. Died of starvation 1318 at Nyköpingshus Castle while imprisoned by his brother, King Birger.
1 CONT Valdemar Magnusson, Duke of Finland in 1302 and Öland 1310. Died of starvation 1318 at Nyköpingshus castle while imprisoned by his brother King Birger.
1 CONT Richeza Magnusdotter of Sweden, abbess of the convent of St. Clara at Stockholm. Died after 1347.
