1 CONT Jon Knutsson (son of Knut Jonsson, tab. 3). Appears for the first time 17.5 1335. Knighted sometime between 1338 and 1342. Was 1344 district chief of Göstrings hd in Östergötland and 1352 district chief of Vedbo hd in Småland. Issued 1350 and 1
1 CONC 356 letters from Aspenäs. Appears for the last time 6.1 1357. Probably died 27.10 1359. Inherited from his father land in Dals hd in Östergötland. Was already married on 31.10 1338 to Helena Laurensdotter, daughter of the knight, councilor and S
1 CONC öderman national lawman Laurens Ulfsson ( Ama ) and his wife Ingrid Anundsdotter. Mrs. Helena was still alive on 27 October 1359.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Bengt Jonsson, 1352-1374. See tab. 6.
1 CONT Ulf Jonsson, 1356-1393, knight, lawman. See tab. 9.
1 CONT Knut Jonsson. Earliest mentioned 28.10 1356. Was ill 27.10 1359, when he at Sanda, now Sånna, in Asby sn in Kinda hd in Östergötland, donated land in Ydre hd in Östergötland to Asby presbytery.
1 CONT Bengta Jonsdotter. Was dead in 1384, as her second husband was then remarried. Owned land in Dals hd in Östergötland. Was 28.10 1356 first married to Sigmund Elofsson (winged arrow), who was still alive in 1363 and was 22.12 1374 remarried to Lar
1 CONC s Filipsson ( Magnus Marinasson's ätt ), who appears in sources 1360-1386, died 3.2 1387 and 9.12 1384 was remarried to Ingrid Jonsdotter.
1 CONT Katarina Jonsdotter. Earliest mentioned 28.10 1356. Lived a widow 8.9 1378. Owned land in Dals hd in Östergötland. Was married to Klemens Petersson (sparre), who appears in 1364 -1371.
0 @NFS4966@ NOTE === !Skandinavisk slektshistorie, Agder (199 ===
1 CONT !Skandinavisk slektshistorie, Agder (1995) side 54: Død 1328. Nevnt i 1295, var da tilstede under riksforhandlingene på hindsgavl kongsgård på Fyn i Danmark. JRS var oså kongens høyeste embetsmann i Bergen, som dengang var Norges hovedstad.
0 @NFS4967@ NOTE === (from "Vossaætter", I, Lars Kindem)  In  ===
1 CONT (from "Vossaætter", I, Lars Kindem)  In the Danish 'noble families' book, there are recorded two families in Norway with the name "Smør, and they are numbered I and II.     Smør was a leading Norwegian family from the middle ages.  The family ow
1 CONC ned much property, mostly in West Norway.  There were several knights, cabinet ministers, and other prominent men among them.     It seems that the line died out on the male side in the 1400's with regent Jon Svaleson Smør, grandson of Jon Halvard
1 CONC son, but continued from the female side in the Rømer family, among others.     The coat of arms was a gold leopard head under a red spear on a blue field. On the helmet are three ostrich plumes and three gold morning stars.     The founder of th
1 CONC e family, Sir Jon Ragnvaldson Smør, created a shield with a diagonal bar for himself in 1297.  The shield is from Sir Jon's seal in 1458. Jon Ragnvaldson Smør, believed born around 1240, died in 1328, inasmuch as a 'soul mass' was held in Old Chu
1 CONC rch in Nordfjord.  He was on the Royal council (of Eirik II 'Priesthalter" and Håkon V), a knight, and one of the important men in the kingdom.  P. A. Munch states that Jon was likely the son of Ragnvald Urka, who attended King Håkon Håkonson 't
1 CONC he old' IV on his voyage to Scotland in 1263 and that Ragnvald was also the father of Aslak Ragnvaldson who lived at the same time as Jon.     Jon was governor in Bergen, Norway's capital at that time.  He was the king's highest ranking official, m
1 CONC aintained order, sat in the courts and collected the king's income.  In 1295 he was at a meeting in the palace in Denmark during peace negotiations between Norway and Denmark.  He helped create a pact between King Eirik II and Archbishop Jørund o
1 CONC f Nideros.  In 1305 he witnessed the dowry of Queen Euphemia.  In 1297 he was in Trøndelag involved in several important cases on his own behalf or as the king's regent.  He is mentioned with other noblemen.     He owned much property in outer Sun
1 CONC defjord which he apparently leased to Munkeliv Cloister for five years in 1300.  He never reclaimed his property. Some believe it was an inheritance from his father and that he actually sold it because he lived mostly in Bergen and as an ambassado
1 CONC r he traveled so much, that he was unable to look after his property as he became older.
