0 @NFS5012@ NOTE === Source: ===
1 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knut_Jonsson_(Aspen%C3%A4s%C3%A4tten)
1 CONT 
1 CONT Knut Jonsson , died 1347 , was a knight, a Swedish councilor and drots to the Swedish kings Birger Magnusson and Magnus Eriksson , as well as lawman in Östergötland 1310-47. He was the son of Johan Filipsson (Aspenäsätten) , resident of Aspenä
1 CONC s . He is sometimes referred to in literature from the 18th and 19th centuries as "Knut Jonsson Blue", due to an assumed but unproven kinship with the mythical Joar Blå and the Blåätten constructed by historians but not documented in sources .
1 CONT 
1 CONT Arms: Knut Jonsson bore a golden lion eagle with a heraldic lily as a tail ornament on a blue shield.
1 CONT Knut Jonsson differed from the great men of his time, in that he kept the same seal until his death in 1346. [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Knut Jonsson is mentioned for the first time in 1292. He was not yet a knight in 1298, but in 1305 he is described as a knight and councillor.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1310 he is referred to as lawman in Östergötland. In 1311 he was Birger Magnusson 's drots , a position he still held in 1314. However, this year, or the following, he was forced to cede the drots dignity to Johan von Brunkow . In both 1315 an
1 CONC d 1316 he is mentioned again as a lawman in Östergötland, as well as in 1330-1345. Signed the Charter of Freedom in 1319 [ 4 ] .
1 CONT 
1 CONT After Nyköping's guest ban , he participated in the rebellion in 1318 against King Birger Magnusson . He becomes guardian and regent for Magnus Eriksson until 1332, when he again becomes drots (for Magnus Eriksson ), a position he loses in 1333 o
1 CONC r 1334.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The last time he appears in life is on September 25, 1345, when he and his wife renew their will. March 18, 1347 he was dead. He was buried in Alvastra Monastery .
1 CONT 
1 CONT Married 5 June 1319 to Katarina Bengtsdotter (Bjälboätten) , daughter of Bengt Magnusson (Folkungaätten's lawman branch) , lawman in Östergötland. Katarina was still alive on 1350-03-12, when she issued letters with Aspenäs as the date place
1 CONC , and gave away her farm Rönnäs, probably located in Småland .
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Filip Knutsson (Aspenäsätten)
1 CONT Birger Knutsson (Aspenäsätten)
1 CONT Magnus Knutsson (Aspenäsätten) , lawman in Östergötland .
1 CONT Jon Knutsson (Aspenäsätten)
1 CONT Cecilia Knutsdotter (Aspenäsätten)
1 CONT Ingeborg Knutsdotter (Aspenäsätten)
1 CONT Notes 
1 CONT ^ SDHK no: 2659, which shows an excellent image of the seal with the lion eagle
1 CONT ^ Swedish seals from the Middle Ages by Brother Emil Hildebrand
1 CONT ^ Clans from Sweden's Middle Ages in Project Runeberg
1 CONT ^ Sd 2199.
1 CONT Further reading 
1 CONT Knut Jonsson (Aspenäsätten) in Swedish Biographical Lexicon (1920)
1 CONT https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Aspen%C3%A4s%C3%A4tten#TAB_7
1 CONT 
1 CONT Knut Jonsson (son of Johan Filipsson, tab. 2). Mentioned for the first time in 1292. Not yet a knight in 1298. Was a knight and councilor in 1305, lawman in Östergötland in 1310 and King Birger's duke in 1311 and again in 1314. Left the duke offi
1 CONC ce around the turn of the year 1314-1315. Mentioned again as a lawman in Östergötland in 1315 and again in 1316, but in 1318 his brother-in-law Bo Nilsson (Night and Day) is a lawman there. Is again drots in 1322 and again in 1333, also again law
1 CONC man in Östergötland in 1330 and again in 1345. Was former drots in 1334. Appears for the last time on 25.9 1345, when he and his wife renew their will. Died 18.3 1347. Several of Mr. Knut's 1343 and others. Letters issued in 1345 have as date pla
1 CONC ce Aspenäs in Malexanders sn in Ydre hd in Östergötl., where at least his wife is stated to have lived. Was married on 5.6 1319 to Katarina Bengtsdotter, daughter of the Östgöta lawman knight Bengt Magnusson ( Folkungaätten's lawman branch) a
1 CONC nd his wife Sigrid. Mrs. Katarina issued letters with Aspenäs as the date place as late as 12.3 1350, when she gave away her farm Rönnäs, located either in Asby sn in Ydre hd in Östergötland or in Agunnaryds sn in Sunnerbo hd in Småland.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Philip Knutsson. Appointed 8.1 1328 by Pope Johannes XXII as canon in Uppsala with expectations of a prebend, even though he already had a canonry and prebend in Linköping. Died before 28.11 1335.
1 CONT Birger Knutsson. Named as living 1342 -12.3 1350
1 CONT Magnus Knutsson, 1335-1366, died trol. last year, knight, lawman. See tab. 4.
1 CONT Jon Knutsson, 1335-1357, knight, district chief. See tab. 5.
1 CONT Cecilia Knutsdotter. Still alive 12.3 1350. Was married to the knight and councilor Bo Nilsson ( Night and Day ), who probably died in 1322. She was betrothed to him as early as 16.5 1306. Called herself a widow after him 25.3 1336 and still aliv
1 CONC e 18.11 1347. Mrs Cecilia owned land in Bråbo and Ydre hd in Östergötland.
1 CONT Ingeborg. Was still alive on 31.1 1333, when she, as a widow of her father, was entered into St. Clare's monastery. Was married to the knight and councilor Erik Valdemarsson ( Folkungaätten ), born 1271 or 1272, died 1330.
0 @NFS5013@ NOTE === Baron Audun Hugleiksson's imprisonment and ultimate execution remain one of the most mysterious and heavily debated scandals in medieval Scandinavian history. ===
1 CONT He Controlled the Military and the Money: Under the previous king, Audun was appointed Fehirde (National Treasurer). He controlled the entire kingdom’s money and was the de facto head of the government's council. King Haakon V feared that Audun h
1 CONC eld more loyalty among the nobility than the crown did.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Because the official court records surrounding his trial were scrubbed, historians have spent centuries piecing together exactly what landed him in the dungeon for three years before his execution. The most accepted theories
1 CONT are:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Financial Fraud: As the Fehirde (Treasury Minister), Audun handled massive international transactions, including a highly famous, enormous financial payout from France to Norway. Accusations likely arose that Audun was pocketing sums for his own pe
1 CONC rsonal gain.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Anti-Royal Ambition: In Norway, building a private stone castle was a privilege strictly reserved for the King and the Church. Audun's construction of a private fortress stood as a massive, arrogant symbol of defiance to royal authority
1 CONT 
1 CONT The local folklore in the area still claims that before Audun took his final, fateful trip to Bergen where he was arrested, he knew he was in danger and threw a solid silver table and all of his gold directly into the deep, icy lake to keep the kin
1 CONC g from taking it!
0 @NFS5014@ NOTE === Historic Mentions  ===
1 CONT Financial Peculation (Embezzlement): In 1295, Audun went to France and promised King Philip the Fair that Norway would provide 100 Viking longships and 50,000 warriors to fight England in exchange for a massive payout. The French paid a huge down p
1 CONC ayment of 6,000 pounds of silver. Audun pocketed the money, returned to Norway, and never sent a single ship. King Haakon V likely used this international fraud as a reason to charge him with corrupting the treasury.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Political Opponent Clearing: Haakon V ascended the throne in July 1299. By August 1299, Audun was arrested. It was a classic, ruthless political purge to eliminate the former administration's top player so the new king could rule without oppositio
1 CONC n.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The Icelandic Annals: This preserved historical chronicle provides the exact date of his execution (December 2, 1302) and explicitly notes that he was hanged rather than beheaded.
1 CONT Bishop Arne’s Saga: A 13th-century saga detailing the life of an Icelandic bishop. It documents accounts of Audun, recording that in the year 1281 he was "highest in the council of the king" and wielded incredible authority over church and stat
1 CONC e matters.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The French Alliance Treaty of 1295: The physical parchment of the treaty Audun signed with the French King still exists in the French national archives. It features Audun's personal handwritten seal, which is the same seal you can see on the memori
1 CONC al at Ålhus in Jølster today
0 @NFS5015@ NOTE === King Magnus Favorite  ===
1 CONT Audun was the King's Golden Boy: Audun started building the castle around 1276. At that exact time, King Magnus was working on his legacy: the Landslov (the first unified National Law Code of Norway). Audun was the king's primary legal architect an
1 CONC d draft writer for these laws.
1 CONT 
1 CONT It was a Reward: King Magnus didn't see the castle as an insult; he saw it as a well-earned reward for his favorite and most brilliant loyal servant. Magnus trusted Audun completely.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Defense for the Realm: Norway's western coast was highly vulnerable. King Magnus viewed a strong stone fortress in that region, controlled by his most loyal advisor, as a brilliant defensive asset for the crown, rather than a threat against it.
1 CONT 
1 CONT While the weak King Eirik and the trusting King Magnus let Audun do whatever he wanted, the dynamic changed completely in 1299 when King Haakon V took the throne.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Haakon V was a hardline, no-nonsense ruler. When he looked at Audun, he did not see a loyal legal genius. He saw a man who owned a private military fortress, controlled the kingdom's money, had private deals with the King of France, and held more r
1 CONC espect among the Norwegian people than the crown itself
1 CONT 
1 CONT To King Haakon V, Audun had become a monster of his own predecessors' making. To save his own authority, the new king had to destroy him.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The "Golden Child" Treatment: Magnus trusted Audun so deeply that he showered him with land, high titles, and let him build a giant stone castle. To a young, observing Haakon, it might have looked like Magnus loved his brilliant advisor more than h
1 CONC is own flesh and blood.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT The Legal Soulmates: King Magnus was obsessed with creating a fair, unified law for his people. Audun was the genius who physically drafted it for him. They worked side-by-side for years on the Landslov
0 @NFS5016@ NOTE === Career Timeline (1273-1299) ===
1 CONT 1273: His first appearance in surviving legal documents, serving as a royal messenger in Bergen. He was named stallare (the king's chief lawman).
1 CONT 1274: He served as a principal architect in writing and implementing Norway's groundbreaking National Law Code (Landslov) under King Magnus the Lawmender.
1 CONT 1276–1286: He designed and built his famous, rare private stone castle, Audunborg, on his family estate.
1 CONT 1277: Promoted to the rank of Lendmann (Baron).
1 CONT 1280: Following the death of King Magnus, the 12-year-old Eirik became king. Audun effectively became the co-ruler and was appointed Fehirde (National Treasurer/Minister of Finance).
1 CONT 1295: He traveled as a high diplomat to France and Scotland, securing a military alliance against England.
1 CONT July 1299: King Eirik died without leaving a male heir. His brother, King Haakon V, assumed the throne.
1 CONT August 1299: Audun was abruptly arrested on the new king's orders, stripped of his noble title, and thrown into a dungeon.
0 @NFS5017@ NOTE === 👑 Why the King Wanted to Humiliate Him ===
1 CONT 🪢 The Execution
1 CONT December 2, 1302: After sitting in prison for over three years, he was publicly hanged at Nordnes in Bergen. Because hanging was reserved for common thieves, the king deliberately intended to humiliate Audun and erase his high noble legacy. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT The Power Threat: Audun had been the co-ruler of the kingdom and effectively ran the treasury. King Haakon wanted to prove that no nobleman was untouchable.
