1 CONT TABLE 5
1 CONT Lars Ulfsson (son of Ulf Holmgersson, tab. 2). Is probably the "scolaris" Lars, who together with his brother Holmger is remembered in 1288 in the will of his "cognatus" Archbishop Magnus Bosson. Safely coated fr. about 1305. Knighted sometime betw
1 CONC een 1312 and 1314. Mentioned as the duchess Ingeborg's council from 1318, as councilor from 1322 and as lawman in Södermanland from 1319. Issued letters in 1330, 1338 and 1342 in Ärnäs in Kärnbo sn, Selebo hd in Södermanland, which he owned ac
1 CONC cording to DS, no. 3130, and where he also appears in letters issued by other persons in 1335 and 1346. It must have been his seat, because his son Holmger later lived there. Still alive in 1350. Chosen burial place in Alvastra monastery. Is, sinc
1 CONC e no other person with this name is known from this time, probably identical to the Lars Ulfsson, who in 1301 was married to Ramborg, who then bore the title of wife and thus had previously been married to a knight. Her father was probably the knig
1 CONC ht and councilor Nils Ingemundsson (lily), whose only known wife mentioned in 1301: was named Ragnfrid. Lars Ulfsson (Ama) was married in 1318 to Ingrid Anundsdotter, who died sometime between 1346 and 1348 and chose her burial place in Alvastra mo
1 CONC nastery and owned estates in Vadsbo hd in Västergötland and in Dals hd in Östergötland. She was one of the heirs of the canon in Uppsala Johan Folkason ( Fånöätten ) and probably a cousin of his mother Ramborg Ingevaldsdotter and therefore a
1 CONC nd with regard to her patronymic, probably the sister of Elena Anundsdotter, who in 1312 mentions Ingevald Estridason ( Hammerstaätten ) as her »consobrinus» , as in 1316 by Magnus and Ulf Gudmarssons ( Ulvåsaätten ) are called »cognatus» a
1 CONC s well as the lawman Knut Magnusson ( Tyrgils Knutsson's family ) and his brother the knight Gustav Magnusson, who also had the right to inherit after Johan Folkason, who in 1318 sealed the first will of Lars Ulfsson and Ingrid Anundsdotter.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children:
1 CONT 
1 CONT 2. Catherine. Entered in 1323 in Vårfruberga monastery.
1 CONT 2. Helena Larsdotter. Appears at the earliest in 1335. Still alive in 1359 and married at the latest on 31.10 1338 to the later knight Jon Knutsson ( Aspenäsätten ), who is mentioned as living 1335-1357.
1 CONT 2. Ulf Ama or Ulf Hama. Appears 1335 - 1342.
1 CONT 2. Holmger Ama. Appears 1342-1359. His seat farm was in 1358 Ärnäs in Kärnbo sn in Selebo hd in Södermanland, and he also owned land in Öknebo hd in the same province. Ärnäs was held after his death by his nephew Bengt Jonsson ( Aspenäsätt
1 CONC en ).
1 CONT 2. Christina. Appears at the earliest in 1335, lived in 1342 and probably still in 1346. Was, after having previously been married to a knight, no later than 1334 married to Birger Algotsson (Hjorthorn, Algot Jonsson's family ), who appears 1326-13
1 CONC 52.
1 CONT 2. Several children. Died at the latest in 1342 and was buried in Alvastra monastery.
0 @NFS5040@ NOTE === Part III http://www.tore-nygaard.com/middelalder/420.htm ===
1 CONT    However, the dominant view among Norwegian historians overlooks two important points. Firstly, that the young Magnus Eriksson and his mother seem to have stayed more in Norway than in Sweden in the 1320s, and secondly and most importantly, the f
1 CONC act that long before 1319 the hirden had primarily become an organization for the country's secular aristocracy, and that this function did not disappear, even when the country got a joint king with a neighboring country. Moreover, there are relati
1 CONC vely clear indications that the court organization was in disintegration during the last half of Håkon V's reign.
1 CONT     In this connection, the large fine issued by King Håkon in June 1308 has caught the interest of historians. The rettarbota is among the most debated documents in Norwegian history, this applies both to its content, motive, legitimacy and its e
1 CONC ffects in the short and long term. The background for the legal remedy was undoubtedly the experiences King Håkon had had in the national government until then. As it is said at the beginning of the legal remedy: "It has been known to all men tha
1 CONC t because of the behavior that various men have had towards our characters, both in our two brothers' childhood and likewise now some time ago ... we are taking our paternal inheritance , the entire kingdom of the king of Norway, under our rule." T
1 CONC he king then decides in detail about the administrative apparatus of the national government and the shepherd, and behind it all was the desire to strengthen the king's control over the national government.
1 CONT     In this connection, it has been particularly pointed out that the king abolished the earl and liegeman names for all time to come. No new lords were to be appointed, and only the king's son and the earl of Orkney were for the future to have th
1 CONC e right to bear the title of earl. Several historians have characterized the rechtsbota as anti-aristocratic and claimed that in the long term it contributed to the Norwegian aristocracy as a nation-bearing group being greatly weakened.
1 CONT     The fact that the king sought to strengthen his own influence did not, however, prevent him at the same time making concessions to the members of the aristocracy in general by opening up the possibility that governorship representatives could i
1 CONC n reality become hereditary. It was a significant concession as the sysla was the aristocracy's main source of income from the monarchy at this time. There were very few squires in 1308, and the crown estate was no longer large enough to entertai
1 CONC n a larger group of squires. There is therefore no basis for characterizing rechtbota as generally anti-aristocratic. The king's intention was apparently to reorganize the government and the aristocracy as a result of fundamental changes in society
1 CONC , and it was a policy in which the king had to play along with the majority of the aristocracy." 1
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT  
1 CONT Henning Sollied: Source-critical investigations concerning some medieval sources, NST Volume VIII (1942), page 116, 121, 129-132, 259-261, 263. Ole Georg Moseng, Erik Opsahl, Gunnar I. Pettersen and Erling Sandmo: Norwegian history I - 750 -1537, T
1 CONC ano Aschehoug 1999, pages 184-187. Lars Løberg: "Hestbøætta", NST Volume XL, booklet 4 (2006), pages 298-304. Mogens Bugge: Our ancestors, no. 826. Bent and Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens' ancestors, page 95.
0 @NFS5041@ NOTE === Part II ===
1 CONT   Jon had a daughter named Ragndid (actually Ragnfrid) and two of the grandchildren were called Gudrun. Both names appear precisely in the Reins family, Ragndid even several times. Ragndid is such a rare name that the mere fact that it occurs withi
1 CONC n the Sudreims family makes it overwhelmingly likely that there must have been some connection between this and the Reins family.
1 CONT     If you now look at the present points in context, it turns out that you will achieve a fully satisfactory explanation both of the mentioned younger kinship assignments and of the name relationships by assuming that Mr. Jon Raud's mother (Gudru
1 CONC n or Ragndid?) was the daughter of Alv av Tornberg and Ingeborg Baardsdatter. This also gives the best possible explanation for the prominent social position of the Sudreims family and for the fact that Mr. Jon's son is chosen as a spouse for Kin
1 CONC g Håkon's daughter."
1 CONT 
1 CONT     In the following, Henning Sollied (excerpt from "Kildekritiske øskninger på nogen middelalslekter" (NST Volume VIII (1942), pages 259-261) argues that Jon's father was Ivar av Skedjuhof. This has later been called into question by others, an
1 CONC d is dealt with somewhat in the biography of his father, Ivar:
1 CONT   "Mr. Jon himself was a loincloth, one can safely take that for granted in Håkon Håkonsson's saga, namely Ivar Nef, but he belonged there. in Hålogaland, is already mentioned in 1198 and became a bailiff at Christmas time 1217. He cannot theref
1 CONC ore be considered as the possible father of Mr. Jon Raud
1 CONT     . beginning of this period, it can be assumed with certainty that several of King Håkon's deserving, high-born men in this time have achieved the dignity of a nobleman, even if this is not directly stated in the
1 CONT     saga Ivar av Skedjuhof has been awarded this award. Skedjuhof in Sørum belonged in the 14th century to Mr. Jon Havtorsson and was also the main farm in his large estate at Romerike! Mr Jon's properties, apart from a few farms in Østfold, wer
1 CONC e not previously known, but can now be determined on the basis of younger property relationships.
1 CONT     Then Mr. Jon's wife, Mrs Birgitte Knutsdatter, was Swedish, and as the properties his father got with his wife, the king's daughter Mrs Agnes, are at least mainly known, there is no reason to doubt under the circumstances that Skedjuhof and th
1 CONC e associated property are an inheritance after the grandfather, Mr. Jon Raud. The fact that Mr. Jon, as the eldest son, chooses Skedjuhof, and not Sudreim, for his main farm in Romerike also clearly indicates that it is the first-mentioned propert
1 CONC y that is the original seat of the family.
1 CONT     According to the present, it can therefore be taken for granted that Mr. Jon Raud Ivarsson is the son of Ivar of Skedjuhof."
1 CONT 
1 CONT     From "Norsk historie I - 750-1537 pages 173-176:
1 CONT   "The herdskråen from the reign of Magnus VI Lagabøte has an archaic feel in that its formal framework goes a long way back to the herdsman's original task as daily warrior retinue, and despite the fact that the herdsman's character had change
1 CONC d significantly. At the end of the 13th century, the country's secular aristocracy went through a formal elite formation, a process that ended with the dissolution of the court organization in the first half of the 14th century.
1 CONT     Magnus Lagabøte decided in 1277 that squires and squires should have the right to the title of lord, and that squires should henceforth be titled "barons" and squires "knights". "Baron" was the title of the English crown vassals, that is, thos
1 CONC e who had land in direct possession of the king, corresponding to the Norwegian feudal lords. "Knight" was the highest rank within the European chivalry that had developed from the 11th century onwards. The original pure cavalry soldier of the Fran
1 CONC kish Empire with armour, shield and sword or lance had gradually become the prototype of the tall, aristocratic member of the vassal class in Europe. The knight was solemnly accepted into a closed community, the "knighthood", through knighthood an
1 CONC d knighthood. In order to qualify for knighthood, one had to undergo military training as an "armor". Beyond the High Middle Ages, the squire class took on a less unequivocal character of being a group of young men who trained to become knights, an
1 CONC d instead became a full-fledged rank class of the same type as the knight class. The fully developed European chivalry in the 12th century therefore consisted of the ranks of knights and squires, with the first as the highest. It was still the cas
1 CONC e that the path to knighthood went via the squire rank, but not all squires became knights.
1 CONT     The order of chivalry contained a knightly ideology which further contributed to raising its members above the rest of the population, not least because there was an increasing emphasis on "burden". The knightly ideology had three main pillars
1 CONC : "Military brotherhood", "ecclesiastical ideals", such as protecting the poor and weak, punishing the wicked and defending the church, and "courtly conduct". For the military brotherhood, a "code of honor" developed, that is to say, rules all memb
1 CONC ers of the knighthood were obliged to follow in war if they were not to lose their honor and thus perhaps be ostracized from the knighthood. The code of honor was common to all members of the knighthood regardless of loyalty ties and therefore form
1 CONC ed a basis for a certain community across conflict lines, not least in war.
1 CONT     The fact that knighthood was introduced in Norway at the end of the 13th century is a clear result of the elite formation that took place in this period, and at the same time shows how Norwegian society was overrun by the common European cultur
1 CONC al and social development of the time. Both parts were also expressed by the fact that non-princely persons in Norway from the end of the 13th century began to use fixed coat of arms in shields and seals.
1 CONT     Another part of the development of the elite was that at the end of the 13th century, the royal power granted the members of the court exemption from taxation in return for their personal military service. The tax exemption was originally linke
1 CONC d to an exemption for persons, but around 1300 it was instead applied to land because the leasehold tax had become a tax on land. The preserved sources are not large enough for us to be able to follow the development in significant detail until, i
1 CONC n the 1370s, Norwegian nobles obtained tax exemption for the entire seat farm, i.e. the land they farmed at their own expense, a privilege they retained for the rest of the late Middle Ages . The tax exemption Norwegian nobles obtained in the Hig
1 CONC h Middle Ages was very modest by European standards. Their Nordic brothers received a similar, but significantly more extensive privilege at about the same time, exemption from royal taxes and imposition of all landed property regardless of rank.
1 CONT     The financial gain from the exemption was so small for Norwegian magnates that the privilege must have mainly had a symbolic-honorary meaning. This gave the magnates in Norway less incentive to increase their private amount of goods, compared t
1 CONC o their colleagues in Denmark and Sweden, as new goods that they did not manage themselves were taxed. Land ownership certainly gave prestige, which was important in the society of the time, but because all their private property was exempt from ro
1 CONC yal taxes, Danish and Swedish magnates could, to a completely different degree than Norwegians, increase their economic resources, and thus also their political ones, by acquiring themselves more goods. The main reason for the difference between No
1 CONC rway and the other Nordic kingdoms in this area must be that the national government had to balance the need to maintain a viable aristocracy as a national elite against the need to preserve sufficient national income. The kingdom in Norway could n
1 CONC ot afford to give up such a large source of income as tax from the aristocracy's collected property, because the country's total area of ​​arable land, the most important capital of the Middle Ages, was relatively small. This also explains th
1 CONC e strong interests the Norwegian secular aristocracy had in the kingdom's administrative apparatus, not least in income from royal service. Correspondingly, the significantly larger land area that the other two Nordic kingdoms had, especially Denma
1 CONC rk, must be the reason why the aristocracy obtained a greater tax privilege and did not have as strong an interest in strengthening the kingdom's administrative apparatus. The aristocracy's freedom from taxation, however, meant a serious financia
1 CONC l burden on the kingdom's finances in both Denmark and Sweden, a burden which in periods led to financial bankruptcy with serious consequences for the national government. And in both countries, measures were taken to limit the revenue loss for th
1 CONC e kingdom as much as possible, without the tax privilege being revoked for that reason.
1 CONT     Despite the difference in the degree of tax freedom for the nobles, there are good reasons to believe that the dissolution of the herd organization and the establishment of a worldly privileged warrior class followed the same main lines in al
1 CONC l three kingdoms, but that the herd disappeared last in Norway. Norwegian historians have generally believed that the shepherd's dissolution in Norway was due to the royal union with Sweden from 1319 onwards, because the task of being the king's bo
1 CONC dyguard and armed entourage disappeared as the joint king mostly stayed outside Norway's borders. In both Denmark and Sweden, on the other hand, the royal herd disappeared already in the 13th century, i.e. without any of the countries being in roya
1 CONC l communion with another country.
0 @NFS5042@ NOTE === Part 1 ===
1 CONT Baron and Governor.
1 CONT Born around 1245.
1 CONT Died around 1312 in Sørum (Sudreim, Skedjuhof), Sørum (AK).
1 CONT 
1 CONT     The Sudreim family's first known man with certainty is the baron Mr. Jon Raud Ivarsson. The surname Rauðr undoubtedly alludes to his hair colour, and there is therefore little reason to discuss the question of possible kinship with others bear
1 CONC ing the same surname. The family from Sørum in Akershus continued in Sweden under the names Roos af Ervalla and Roos af Hjelmsäter. After the Hårfagres clan had died out on the male side, the family were heirs to the throne of Norway. However, t
1 CONC hey renounced the right of inheritance.
1 CONT 
1 CONT     The source material is "poor" in the 13th century so we do not know who was the father of Baron Jon Raud Sudreim.
1 CONT 
1 CONT     I have previously assumed that the governor of Hedmark, Ivar av Skejuhof, was his father, among other things. a. based on Henning Sollied "Source-critical investigations relating to some medieval studies", NST Volume VIII (1942).
1 CONT 
1 CONT     After a discussion on the Digital Archive's user forum in 2015, I have come to the conclusion that what we do not know is probably more about his father than that his name was Ivar.
1 CONT     Parts of the discussion are included in the biography of his father.
1 CONT 
1 CONT     I have also rejected the hypothesis that his mother was the daughter of Alv Erlingsson de av Tomberg and Ingeborg Baardsdatter from Rein.
1 CONT 
1 CONT     From Henning Sollied's "Source-critical investigations regarding noge middelvalekter", NST Volume VIII (1942), pages 116-117:
1 CONT   "Mr. Jon is mentioned in the sources for the first time as co-sealer of the armistice agreement with Denmark, concluded at Hindsgavl, Funen, 25/09/1295. He was then a knight and is mentioned first among the gentlemen who co-seal the agreement ("R
1 CONC egesta Norvegica" II, no. 790):
1 CONT     Summary:
1 CONT   "Treaty of armistice between King Erik Menved of Denmark, his brother Kristoffer and the king's friends and subjects on the one side , and King Eirik Magnusson of Norway and his friends and subjects on the other: The truce shall last until 15 day
1 CONC s after Jonsok 1298, when the parties shall meet for new sessions. Until this time, both parties shall be able to freely travel through each other's realms, except for the outlaws. Merchants from both countries must be able to freely travel to eac
1 CONC h other and trade, and they must not be subject to extra fees. The Norwegian king shall have free right of use to his maternal inheritance in Denmark. The Danish outlaws who escaped to Norway should be able to freely return to Denmark, and there ge
1 CONC t their goods back. But those who have been sentenced to be men to the Danish king's father shall not appear before the king, Duke Kristoffer or Duke Valdemar. The Norwegian king shall have the right to rule over the castles of Hjelm and Hunehals
1 CONC , and no new fortress shall be built during the standstill period to the detriment of either party, or to hinder the merchants.
1 CONT     Guarantors from the Norwegian side: Jørund, Archbishop of Nidaros, Bishops Narve of Bergen and Øyvind of Oslo, Knights Bjarne [Erlingsson] of Bjarkøy, Tore Håkonsson, Jon Brynjolvsson, Isak Gautesson, Erlend Åmundesson, Vidkunn Erlingsson
1 CONC , Bjarne Lodinsson (juris civilis professor). , Guttorm Gydasson, Jon Ragnvaldsson, Gudleik Viljalmsson, Torvald Toresson, Elling Davidsøn, Jon Ivarsson, Torlack Skenck, Erland Arnesøn, Sigvard av Leirhol, Ogmund Sigurdsson, Esbern Rinkij, Ander
1 CONC s Leui and Effuind Islacksøn."
1 CONT 
1 CONT     He also co-seals King Erik's letter of privilege for Hamburg citizens of 31 July 1296 (DN V 33, "Regesta Norvegica" II, no. 823).
1 CONT     Furthermore, he is co-issuer of a letter dated 25/02/1299 concerning the farm Frogner Store, Ullensaker, (DN II 47, "Regesta Norvegica" II no. 967).
1 CONT 
1 CONT     In 1302, his son Havtore was betrothed to virgin Agnes (Isl. Ann. IV, V) and at the latest on this occasion, Mr. Jon may have been appointed baron,
1 CONT     as such he is mentioned as such already on 09.09.1302 when he, among other things, a. together with his son-in-law, Mr. Sebjørn Helgesson co-sealed King Håkon's decree on the royal succession and the national administration (NGL III page 46
1 CONC , "Regesta Norvegica" III, no. 53):
1 CONT   "Corporate penalty from King Håkon Magnusson: Has, at the people's request, investigated King Magnus' law book and found that it does not in all parts contain what is necessary for the kingdom, especially regarding the royal succession. Therefor
1 CONC e establishes a new succession law, where a legitimate daughter's son is moved up into the 3rd inheritance class, a legitimate daughter into the 7th, and an illegitimate daughter's legitimate son into the 9th (cf. RN II no. 111 and ovf no. 8).
1 CONT     In order for the kingdom to avoid the damage it suffered during Håkon's and his brother Eirik's childhood, the king further establishes rules for guardianship when the king is a child. The board of trustees shall consist of 12 men, of whom 4
1 CONC , including the chancellor and the marquess, shall reside permanently in the royal estate. Two bishops together with the 4 will have the crown's gold and silver in custody. Detailed rules are given for how the 12 are to govern the kingdom and distr
1 CONC ibute the tasks between them, among other things when it comes to peace in the country, the relationship with the cattle herder, the governors and other ombudsmen, organization of the mint and management of the kingdom's property. It will be forbid
1 CONC den to increase nominal fines and give larger fines than those stipulated in Hirdskråen, and to allow foreign men to have permanent residence in the kingdom. Finally, rules are given for the treatment of the deceased king's widow, his daughter an
1 CONC d her eventual husband. The king's age of authority is set at 20 years. A special oath, which the guardians must take, is reproduced.
1 CONT     The rettarboten is issued with the advice and consent of Jørund, Archbishop of Nidaros, Arne, Bishop of Stavanger, Narve, Bishop of Bergen, Øyvind, Bishop of Oslo and Torstein, Bishop of Hamar and by the laymen Mr. Bjarne Erlingsson, Mr. Ulvh
1 CONC edin Bjarnesson, Mr. Isak Gautesson, Mr. Jon Ivarsson, Mr. Sæbjørn Helgesson and Mr. Åke chancellor, of the rest of the council and all the best men who were present.
1 CONT     Sealed by the king and the aforesaid bishops and laymen."
1 CONT 
1 CONT     In a letter of 14.09.1307 (DN I 111, «Regesta Norvegica» III, no. 426) King Håkon gave him 11⁄2 øresbol in the farm Duflá (Doblaug) at Hedmark.
1 CONT 
1 CONT     In the summer of 1309, Mr. Jon present in St. Mary's Church in Oslo at the betrothal of King Håkon's legitimate daughter, maiden Ingeborg, to Junker Magnus Birgersson, son of King Birger of Sweden.
1 CONT     Finally, he co-seals a change of spouse dated 29/06/1312 (DN IV 95, "Regesta Norvegica" III, no. 769) between St. Mary's Church in Oslo and Hovedø monastery.
1 CONT 
1 CONT     Later he does not appear in the sources, and presumably died not long after. According to several of the Icelandic annals (Isl. ann. V, VIII, IX, X), Mr. Jon must have died in 1306, but this is not correct. Because of the children's lifespans
1 CONC , he must be assumed to have been born around 1245 at the latest. Mr. Jon's wife is not mentioned in the sources, but she too probably belonged to a prominent family, since one of the sons could be chosen as a spouse for the king's daughter."
1 CONT 
1 CONT     Henning Sollied argues in "Kiddekriticske øskninger på nogen middelalslekter" (NST Volume VIII (1942), pages 129-132) that Mr. Jon must be assumed to be the daughter's son of the landman Alv av Tornberg and Ingeborg Baardsdatter of the Rein
1 CONC s family - something that is disputed - among others by Lars Løberg in "Hestbøætta" (NST Volume XL, booklet 4 (2006), pages 298-304). See also my biography of Agnes Håkonsdatter's presumed maternal grandfather, Sigurd Ivarsson Hestbø:
1 CONT   "In a letter of 18.12.1330 (DN VII 134, "Regesta Norvegica" IV, no. 784) the Pope grants a dispensation for the marriage between Torvald Torvaldsson, son of Mr. Torvald Toresson and Mrs Ragndid Jonsdatter, and Æsa Haakonsdatter, daughter of th
1 CONC e knight and councilor Mr. Haakon Toresson to Manvik in Brunlanes and Leikvang in Eidsberg as the parties were related in the 4th degree.
1 CONT     From this, it is possible to draw closer conclusions about the origin of Sudreimsätten. Already the goods conditions make it fairly certain that Mr. Torvald Toresson's family must be searched in Hjaltland. The Herdis name is also practically u
1 CONC nknown in Norway. On the other hand, Æsa's mother is certainly Danish by birth. It is therefore predominantly likely that it is Mrs Ragndid Jonsdatter and Mr. Haakon Toresson who is related. Now there is also another papal marriage license withi
1 CONC n their immediate family, as Mrs Ragndid's nephew Mr. Sigurd Havtorsson on 29/09/1342 (DN VI 166) received permission to marry Mr. Håkon's sister's daughter Ingeborg Erlingsdatter, daughter of Drottseten hr. Erling Vidkunnsson and Mrs Elin Toresda
1 CONC tter. Also Mr. Sigurd and Ingeborg are stated to be close friends. There can therefore be no doubt that Mrs Ragndid and Mr. Havtore was the third son of Mr. Håkon and Mrs Elin. This again means that Mr. Jon Raud Ivarsson, or his wife, must be cous
1 CONC ins ​​either of Mr. Håkon and Mrs Elin's father, the baron Mr. Tore Håkonsson (Biskopsson) of Leikvang or of their mother, who was also called Mrs Ingeborg Erlingsdatter. There are, of course, several possibilities. About Mr. Tore's ancestor
1 CONC s are only known that he was the son of Archbishop Håkon and there is in any case nothing to indicate that he was of a particularly prominent family, it would then be the son's distinguished marriage. The relationship of Mr. Tore's wife, Mrs. Inge
1 CONC borg Erlingsdatter, is, on the other hand, better known. She was the sister of the famous earl Mr. Alv Erlingsson ("Milder-Alv") and daughter of the baron Mr. Erling Alvsson to Tornberg (Tanberg) in Ringerike who was again the son of the squire Al
1 CONC v of Tornberg and Ingeborg Baardsdatter of the Reins family, King Inge and Duke Skule's sister.
