0 @NFS5062@ NOTE === Sources ===
1 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandbro%C3%A4tten
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sandbroätten or Styrbjörnssönernas ätt (top from left) are modern conventional names for a Swedish medieval savior family that was well known during the 14th century. The name comes from the family estate Sandbro in Björklinge parish . [ 1 ] T
1 CONC he family died out before Sweden's Riddarhus was founded in 1625, which is why it was never introduced there as a form of nobility .
1 CONT 
1 CONT Arms: split shield with point from the left, sometimes the lower edge of the point is almost, or completely straight horizontal, sometimes the underside of the point goes diagonally upwards, which can be seen on SDHK no: 3797 with the arms of fou
1 CONC r contemporary sigilants from the family: Torsten Styrbjörnsson, dennes brothers Johannes, Olof, and Greger. [ 2 ]
1 CONT 
1 CONT History
1 CONT Sandbroätten's seat was Sandbro in Björklinge parish , Norunda county , which in the Middle Ages was one of Uppland's largest counties, and with a large element of salvage land . [ 1 ] The two medieval stone houses (fortified castles) Lindholm a
1 CONC t the northern tip of Langsjon and Nysas at the eastern shore of Långsjön , two kilometers apart, also belonged to the family . [ 3 ] [ 4 ]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Family tree
1 CONT Styrbjörn Torstensson:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Rahmqvist, Sigurd, Sandbroätten, Journal of Personal History, Vol. 74 (1978), pp. 47-79
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT ^ [ a b c ] Rahmqvist, Sigurd, Sandbroätten, Personhistorisk Tidskrift, Vol. 74 (1978), pp. 47-79
1 CONT ^ SDHK no: 3797 with arms of four sigillants from the family
1 CONT ^ Rahmqvist, Sigurd; Sandbroätten, an Uppland salvation family and its estate. PhD in 1978
1 CONT ^ Castles and fortifications in medieval Sweden by Christian Lovén , King. Academy of History, History and Antiquities, 1999, ISSN 0083-6761
1 CONT ^ SDHK no: 5464
1 CONT ^ Letters from the ashes: the miscellaneous letters lost in the castle fire in 1697 or in other ways according to older archive lists, Johan Peringskiöld's notes etc. by Hans Gillingstam , Swedish National Archives, 1996, Writings published by th
1 CONC e Swedish National Archives, National Archives Stockholm, ISSN 0346-8488
1 CONT ^ [ a b ] Händelö farm - REPORT FROM THE NATIONAL QUARRY OFFICE, ARCHAEOLOGICAL ASSIGNMENT ACTIVITIES (UV)
1 CONT ^ Bielke of Åkerå at adelsvapen.com
1 CONT ^ Elgenstierna, Swedish nobility genealogical charts I, 1925 art. Bielke of Åkerö, Table 11 p. 356
1 CONT ^ Anthoni, Finland's medieval salvation and 16th-century nobility, 1970 p. 62, 91 note 1
