0 @NFS5087@ NOTE === Research on Jon Gautsson Ornes ===
1 CONT Recent research (starting with Magerøy 1988) has almost conclusively proven that Jon Gautsson was from Ornes in Solvorn parish (Sogn og Fjordane), not from Ænes in Kvinnherad parish (Hordaland). While some trees have asserted he was married to N
1 CONC N Arnbjørnsdatter Ambe, there is no historical record for this. Three sons are known: Arnbjørn, Gaut, and Åskjell, all of whom became prominent men.  A possible fourth son, who appears in many trees, is Gunbjørn, though there appears to be litt
1 CONC le information to support this.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The current configuration of this line reflects what is supported via historical-based research. Other wives, and other children, are speculative at best, or, at worst, spurious/fictive. I've kept them in a separate group, but likely these need t
1 CONC o be revised, or their relationships to Jon Gautsson removed.
0 @NFS5088@ NOTE ===  Jon og broren Munan var med kong Magnus ===
1 CONT  Jon og broren Munan var med kong Magnus som skipshøvedsmen n i slaget ved Nordnes i Bergen. Magnus ble kronet som nors k konge i 1164 og falt i slaget ved Fimreite i 1184. Jon fa lt formentlig under  slaget 1181 ved Nordnes. Barn: 1. Arnb jørn (
1 CONC 1175), baglerhøvding, lendermann. 2. Aaskell (1180) , biskop. 3. Gaute (1180 -1270).
0 @NFS5089@ NOTE === Source information about JON GAUTSEN from Urnes  English translation ===
1 CONT [posted by MarciaLee; dp edited for reading clarity]
1 CONT Jon and his brother Munan were also Lendermen, like their father. And like most of the great families at the time, they were also involved Magnus Erlingsen in the battle against King Sverre. Both Jon and Munan are mentioned as ship commanders in th
1 CONC e Battle of Nordnes i
1 CONT Bjørgvin 1181. And it is likely that Jon got his way there, because after the battle nothing more is heard of him, while Munan was still one of Magnus' men. In the battle at  Norefjorden in Sogn in 1184 where Magnus Erlingsen fell, was the Munan w
1 CONC ho steered the ship "Reinen". He is not specifically mentioned among the chiefs who fell in with Magnus, but we don't hear anything about him later, so it's probably most likely that he got his way there, he too, thinks Rasmus Høyland in "Old fami
1 CONC lies in Sunnhordland".
1 CONT Odd Handegård points in Our joint family history p. 180 that it is "probable that Munan survived the bloody the battles at Nordnes (1181) and at Fimreite (1184), but no descendants are mentioned after him. Jon also participated the battle of Nordn
1 CONC es in 1181 where around 360 men were killed on both sides in this battle between Magnus Erlingson and Swear. As Jon is not mentioned in the saga literature after 1181, it has been assumed that he was killed at Nordnes. However, there is no document
1 CONC ation beyond this."
1 CONT [dp adds--from: https://lokalhistoriewiki.no/wiki/Sunnhordlands%C3%A6tter#Galt%C3%A6tta
