0 @NFS5107@ NOTE === Lendermann, Borgarsyssel fra 1275. ===
1 CONT Lendermann, Borgarsyssel fra 1275.  Lendermann was a title of nobility in medieval Norway, denoting an official entitled to receive or collect taxes for royal lands. A lendermann was often also an adviser to the king.  After 1277, the title 'baron
1 CONC ' began to be used for the office. The title 'lendermann' or 'lendmann' was not used after 1308.
0 @NFS5108@ NOTE === Brief Review ===
1 CONT Erling Alvsson (ca. 1230-1283) was a feudal lord and baron from 1277. He belonged to the powerful family with a seat Tanberg farm Tanberg (Tornberg) on Ringerike. He was the son of lenderman Elf Erlingsson (older) and Ingeborg Baardsdatter was Skul
1 CONC e Bardsson sister. His father attended one of Skule in revolt against the nearest Håkon. It prevented not that Erling joined up as one of the Empire's most influential chiefs after his father's death. ERLING was ruler over the entire Østland an
1 CONC d one of the most powerful noblemen in the country.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Erling took part in Magnus Lagabøtes wed Ingeborg Plovpenningsdatter, and the subsequent coronation ceremony in Bergen in 1261. In 1263 he participated as one of the leading landed men on the Earl Håkonsson skottetog. He fought at the Battle of L
1 CONC args, and was by King Hakon's side when he died in Orkney same winter. Along with Andres lenderman Plytt led Erling with the king's ship the same back to Bergen in the spring of 1264.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Still, it was Magnus Lawmender he emerged as the most influential, the two may have been personal friends. He appears at the king's side in several documents and guaranteed sættargjerden in Tønsberg on the king's side in 1277. After King Magnus
1 CONC ' death in 1280 he was one of the barons who uttøvde the royal anti-ecclesiastical policy that made Eirik Magnusson got the nickname "the Priest Hater".
1 CONT 
1 CONT In the late 1270's was Erling the lucrative position of governor in the Civil Governor and held mainly in Sarpsborg in the rest of his life. When King Valdemar Birgersson of Sweden had to flee from his kingdom in 1276, after his brother, Magnus Bir
1 CONC gersson (later Ladulås) had rebelled, he fled to Sarpsborg and had Erling's protection. Later attended Erling with his son, Alf Erlingsson (younger) in the negotiations between the two brothers.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Erling must have had a strong independent position as governor. When he died in 1283 because his son inherited his position Elf, which actually went against national law.
1 CONT Last Changed: November 29, 2022
1 CONT 
1 CONT J
1 CONT John Hedman
1 CONT SEE ALL CHANGES
