0 @NFS5121@ NOTE === Sources ===
1 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johan_Filipsson_(Aspen%C3%A4s%C3%A4tten)
1 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Filipson-1
1 CONT https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Aspen%C3%A4s%C3%A4tten#TAB_7
1 CONT  
1 CONT Johan Filipsson , died August 20, 1280 ( executed ), was a Swedish knight , magnate and commoner from Östergötland .
1 CONT 
1 CONT Johan Filipsson was probably the son of Filip Birgersson (Aspenäsätten) and Cecilia Knutsdotter (Bjälboätten) . He is mentioned, then already a knight, for the first time in 1272 in a deed of gift issued by King Valdemar Birgersson . He is some
1 CONC times called "Johan Filipsson Blue" in literature from the 18th and 19th centuries, due to an assumed but unproven relationship with the mythical Joar Blå and the Blåätten constructed by historians, but not proven in sources .
1 CONT 
1 CONT Johan Filipsson first fell out with King Valdemar Birgersson, reconciled with him with the help of the Danish king, but later helped Valdemar's brother Magnus Ladulås to the throne. He was involved in the Folkunga Uprising and was therefore impris
1 CONC oned by Magnus Ladulås at Gälakvist Castle in Skara , and was executed after a row at Stortorget , Stockholm .
1 CONT 
1 CONT He was married to Ingeborg Svantepolksdotter , daughter of Svantepolk Knutsson .
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT Knut Jonsson (Aspenäsätten)
1 CONT Sources 
1 CONT Johan Filipsson (Aspenäsätten) in Swedish Biographical Lexicon (1920)
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT ↑ 1.0 1.1 Wernstedt, 1957, p. 9
1 CONT ↑ 2.0 2.1 SBL online edition, Johan Filipsson, sighted 1/12 2018
1 CONT Wernstedt, F. (1957) Äldre Svenska frälsesläkter, vol. I.1. Stockholm: Riddarhusdirektionen.
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Aspenäsätten, Adelsvapen-Wiki
1 CONT "Johan Filipsson (Aspenäsätten)," Wikipedia, (hämtad juni 7, 2016
1 CONT 
1 CONT TABLE 2
1 CONT Johan Filipsson (son of Filip, tab. 1). Appears for the first time 22.3 1272 in Gälakvist by Skara in the company of King Valdemar Birgersson as dominus. Was one of the leaders of the confederation of magnates against King Magnus and was execute
1 CONC d on 20 August 1280 together with his brother Birger, nephew Johan Karlsson ( Fånöätten ) and several others. Together with his brother Birger, managed Ymseborg in Bäcks sn in Vadsbo hd in Västergötland, and the brothers also owned the salmo
1 CONC n fishery in Klarälven in Kils hd in Värmland. Was married to Ingeborg Svantepolksdotter, who was the daughter of the Östgöta lawman knight Svantepolk Knutsson ( Svantepolk Knutsson's ätt ) and probably Bengta, daughter of Earl Folke's son Sun
1 CONC e Folkason , then remarried to the knight Tune Anundsson ( Vingätten ) and died 22.2 1341.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Knut Jonsson, 1292-1345, knight, councillor, lawman, drots. See tab. 3.
1 CONT ? Birgitta Jonsdotter. Was on 10.8 1325 married for a long time to the Riksdag knight Peter Ragvaldsson (three squares), who first appears in the sources in 1296 and was still alive in 1326. Since the spouses on the above-mentioned date call the Du
1 CONC ke Knut Jonsson (tab. 3) »gener et cognatus», it has been assumed , that she was his sister. However, one cannot rule out the possibility that Birgitta was the daughter of Johan Birgersson, who in a 1310 authenticated letter without specified dat
1 CONC e is said to have mortgaged 2 örtugland of land in Österröra village in Västra Ryds sn in Bro hd in Uppland to Uppsala Cathedral. In the same village, the husband and wife of the couple also mortgaged in 1322 16 örtugland, belonging to Peter R
1 CONC agvaldsson, to Uppsala Cathedral, with his consent, and in 1325 Peter Ragvaldsson and his wife Drotsen Knut Jonsson authorized at the ting to announce their bequest of 1 markland of land in Österröra to the same cathedral building fund. If one as
1 CONC sumes with the support of this that Birgitta was the daughter of Johan Birgersson, he may have been the son of Birger Filipsson (tab. 1), but before he married Ulfhild Magnusdotter. In addition to patronymics and the spouses' kinship with Knut Jons
1 CONC son, the following property holdings could speak for this. In 1279, Birger donated a nearby farm Tibble to Fogdö monastery, which however was not allowed to possess the farm in peace. Birgitta also owned land in a Tibble, the location of which i
1 CONC s not specified in the source either. A presumption that it would be the same Tibble that Birger owned is close at hand. Birgitta was the widow of Peter Ragvaldsson 31.5 1328, when she issued a letter at Nynäs in Bälinge sn in Rönö hd in Söder
1 CONC manland, and was dead 1.8 1346. Owned mills in Torshälla in Södermanland, which she had exchanged for land in Närke, probably in Grimstens hd. It is likely that the lion eagle coat of arms mentioned in the introduction on Sten Bielke's canvas be
1 CONC longs to Birgitta's family, because this coat of arms, among other combinations, is presented three times in the drawing next to another, which represents three red bars arranged in a field of gold, apparently her husband's coat of arms.
