1 CONT Great Grandfather
0 @NFS5135@ NOTE === If you think you're related to this King, I'm sorry to burst your bubble, but.... you're not. ===
1 CONT I found a connection to this king in my tree, and didn't believe it for a minute. I just spend a couple of days proving I was right. I can also show how it's highly unlikely for anyone outside the accepted royal lines to claim a family connection
1 CONC . You can read my investigation here https://ariks.substack.com/p/the-pencil-mark-that-made-me-a-descendant
1 CONT 
1 CONT But the main thing to understand is that there's a 180-year gap in Norwegian records that coincides with the onset of the Black Death Plague, which killed as much as two thirds of the population and depopulated whole regions. Record keeping stoppe
1 CONC d entirely excepted in the major administrative and ecclesiastical centers. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT A lot of people with public trees claim a connection by way of one of Håkon IV's illegitimate children with Kanga the Younger, a daughter said to be named Thoron. Yes she existed, and yes there is documentary proof that a son and grandson of Thoro
1 CONC n were considered extended family of the king, and granted the farm Berg in Stor-Elvdal. But all of those records pre-date the Black Death, and records about Elvdal stop entirely in 1350 and don't resume until about 1530, with completely differen
1 CONC t people and farm names. There's a lot more to this that I cover in the essay, but the main thing to know is this: Someone else's family tree isn't evidence, there is no evidence that the lines descended from the illegitimate children survived to t
1 CONC he 16th Century, and probably didn't.
