0 @NFS5177@ NOTE === Gregors Birgersson in Swedish Wikipedia ===
1 CONT Gregers Birgersson , Mr. Gregers , knight, first mentioned in 1272 , died January 15, 1276 was a Swedish landowner in Ängsö , in Västmanland and Arnö, now Biskops-Arnö in Uppland . He was the son of Earl Birger and born out of wedlock by an un
1 CONC known woman. He is considered the progenitor of the family known in historical literature as Bjälboätten's illegitimate branch (sometimes Folkungaätten's illegitimate branch), which was previously assumed to be identical to the Läma family .
1 CONT 
1 CONT The information that Earl Birger had a son born out of wedlock is found in a now-lost Icelandic annal that the 17th-century Danish historian Otto Sperling quoted. It is confirmed by a document on March 27, 1272, issued by » G quondam ducis filiu
1 CONC s [ 1 ] , where he exchanged goods in Baldastad in Närke, for Ängsö in Västmanland. [ 2 ]
1 CONT [1] Bengt Hildebrand & Hans Gillingstam:  Gregers Birgersson in Swedish Biographical Lexicon
1 CONT [2]SDHK: 918
1 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregers_Birgersson#cite_note-1
0 @NFS5178@ NOTE === Bjälboätten ===
1 CONT Bjälboättens oäkta gren tidigare kallad Folkungaättens oäkta gren är benämningar på en svensk högfrälsesläkt som härstammade från Bjälboätten via Birger jarls utomäktenskapliga son Gregers Birgersson (död 15 januari 1276). Ätte
1 CONC n är utgrenad ur Bjälboätten.
0 @NFS5179@ NOTE === Burial ===
1 CONT Gregers was buried at the Franciscans in Uppsala. In today's Uppsala, the remains of the monastery are located in the Torget neighborhood at Klostergatan/Östra Ågatan. The monastery has been excavated and several graves have been found.
