0 @NFS5258@ NOTE === Biografi ===
1 CONT Hon placerades i Vreta kloster, tillhörande Benediktinorden, för att utbildas. Från klostret blev hon enligt folkvisan bortrövad av lagmannen i Östergötland Lars Petersson, och rymde med honom till Norge. Väl i Norge avled han efter ett pa
1 CONC r år.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Då hon återvände till Sverige gifte hon sig med Svantepolk Knutsson, som var frilloson till den danske hertigen Knut Valdemarsson.
0 @NFS5259@ NOTE === Sources: ===
1 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birger_Brosa
1 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SWEDISH%20NOBILITY.htm#BirgerBrosadied1202
1 CONT 
1 CONT 1.         BIRGER "Brosa" (-9 Jan 1202).  He is named as the son of Bengt in the 14th century Varnem Abbey genealogy[10].  Jarl in Sweden 1174.  “Byrgerus dux magnus et Karolus fratres eius” witnessed a charter dated to [1167/85] under whic
1 CONC h “Kanutus…Rex Sweorum atque Gotthorum” donated property to the monks of Juleta kloster[11].  “…B. dux…M. frater ducis B et filius eius Eskillus…” witnessed a charter dated to [1167/99] under which “Kanutus…Sweorum rex” donate
1 CONC d property to the monks of Juleta kloster[12].  “Byrgo Sweorum et Guttorum dux…Philippus frater meus, Magnus et Karolus fratres ducis…” witnessed a charter dated to [1167/99] under which “K…Sweorum rex filius Herjcjs itjdem regis” don
1 CONC ated property to Nydala kloster[13].  The Saga of King Sverre records the death of "Earl Birgi Brosa" in the same year as Sverre King of Norway[14].  The Icelandic Annals record the death in 1202 of "Birgerus comes in Suecia"[15].  m (after 1161) a
1 CONC s her third husband, BRIGIDA of Norway, widow firstly of KARL Sunasson Jarl in Västergötland, and secondly of MAGNUS Henriksson, King in Östergötland, daughter of HARALD "Gille" King of Norway & his [first/second] wife --- (-[Riseberga Abbey] 2
1 CONC 2 Oct ----).  Snorre records that "King Harald Gille's daughter Brigida" was first married to "the Swedish king Inge Halsteinson, and afterwards to Earl Karl Sonason, and then to the Swedish king Magnus", noting that she and King Inge Haraldson wer
1 CONC e cousins by the mother's side, and "at last [she] married Earl Birger Brose"[16].  It is not chronologically possible for Brigida to have married Inge Halsteinson King of Sweden, who died in 1118.  She must have been an infant at the time of her m
1 CONC arriage to Karl Sunasson.  The Saga of King Sverre records that "Earl Birgi Brossa" had married "Brigit", the sister of King Sverre's father[17].  Birger & his wife had seven children: 
1 CONT 
1 CONT a)         FILIP Birgersson (-killed in battle [Akerhus] 4 Jan 1200).
1 CONT b)         KNUT Birgersson (-killed in battle Lena 31 Jan 1208). 
1 CONT c)         INGEGÄRD Birgersdotter (-after 1210). 
1 CONT d)         FOLKE Birgersson (-killed in battle Gestilren 17 Jul 1210).
1 CONT e)         MAGNUS Birgersson .  Snorre names (in order) "Earl Philip, Earl Knut, Folke and Magnus" as the four sons of "Earl Birger Brose" & his wife[28]. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT f)          KRISTINA .  Snorre names (in order) "Ingegerd…married to the Swedish king Sorkver [and] a second daughter…Kristin and a third Margaret" as the daughters of "Earl Birger Brose" & his wife[29]. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT g)         MARGARETA .  Snorre names (in order) "Ingegerd…married to the Swedish king Sorkver [and] a second daughter…Kristin and a third Margaret" as the daughters of "Earl Birger Brose" & his wife[30]. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birger Brosa , died 9 January 1202 on Visingsö , was a Swedish earl 1174-1202 and bore a lily as a coat of arms. The nickname "Brosa", said to mean 'the smiling one', is only known from West Norse sources from the 13th and 14th centuries. He is re
1 CONC ferred to in the 1180s as dux Sweorum [ 2 ] , but in most sources as Sweorum et Guttorum Dux . [ 3 ]
1 CONT 
1 CONT It is considered that he was the first earl for the entire Kingdom of Sweden ( Karl Sverkersson had two earls, Ulf and Guttorm) and he was the most powerful man of his time in Östergötland and owned several farms in Östergötland, Närke and Vä
1 CONC rmland. It is also likely that he had farms in Södermanland, as he had patronage rights to Sankt Eskil's church in Södermanland. Birger Brosa was a big donor to Riseberga monastery .
1 CONT 
1 CONT He seems to have been a person with access to significant power resources, who managed to keep peace for a long period in Sweden. After his death, power feuds erupted almost immediately. His date of death is known from a psalter in the British Muse
1 CONC um . [ 4 ]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birger Brosa was the son of Bengt Snivil and married before 1170 Birgitta Haraldsdotter . One of his brothers was Magnus Minnisköld , father of earl Birger .
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Content
1 CONT 1	Birger Brosa's coat of arms
1 CONT 2	Birger Brosa's family
1 CONT 3	Birger Brosa in the media
1 CONT 4	References
1 CONT 4.1	Notes
1 CONT 4.2	Sources
1 CONT Birger Brosa's coat of arms 
1 CONT Birger Brosa carried a lily in his coat of arms, which is known from Birger Brosa's seal from about 1160, and has formed an example for the lily branch of Bjälboätten.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The Swedish herald Jan Raneke's blazon for Birger Brosa's shield reads: "A white stave lily on a red field". [ 1 ]
1 CONT 
1 CONT This opinion has been refuted by Professor Bo Johnson Theutenberg , author of the book From the Holy Sepulcher to Västergötland , who believes that it is a French lily , and believes that it connects to Sigtrygg Bengtsson's coat of arms. [ 5 ]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birger Brosa's family 
1 CONT According to Heimskringla , Birger had the following children in his marriage to the Norwegian king's daughter Birgitta Haraldsdotter [ 4 ] :
1 CONT 
1 CONT Filip , earl to the Norwegian king Sverre Sigurdsson , fell in January 1200.
1 CONT Knut , earl of Sweden, fell on 31 January 1208 in the battle of Lena .
1 CONT Folke, probably, but not with certainty, identical with Folke , earl of Sweden, fell 17 July 1210 in the battle of Gestilren . [ 6 ] based on circumstantial evidence provided by e.g. Sten Karlsson , who are supported by Hans Gillingstam . [ 4 ] Cri
1 CONC ticism against this assumption includes i.a. that Snorre Sturlassonmentions four sons of Birger Brosa: earl Filip, earl Knut, Folke and Magnus, in the story this Folke Birgersson lacks the title of earl in contrast to both older brothers, and it i
1 CONC s stated as strange that Snorri would not know Sweden's foremost individual man after Birger Brosa dead, especially if earl Folke was King Harald Gilles's granddaughter. [ 7 ]
1 CONT Magnus , known only from the Heimskringla and a psaltery
1 CONT Ingegärd , Queen of Sweden, married to Sverker the Younger .
1 CONT Kristina , known only from Heimskringla.
1 CONT Margareta , known only from the Heimskringla and a psaltery.
1 CONT Birger Brosa in the media 
1 CONT In the film adaptation of the Arn books , Birger Brosa is played by Stellan Skarsgård . Arn and Birger are said to be of the Folkunga family, although nowadays the name Bjälboätten is preferred for this family to avoid confusion with the politic
1 CONC al party Folkungarna . In the Arn books, Magnus Minnisköld is not portrayed as his brother, but as the son of his nephew Arn.
1 CONT 
1 CONT References 
1 CONT Notes 
1 CONT ^ [ a b ] Raneke, Jan (1982-1985). Swedish medieval weapons . Bodafors: Doxa. page 786. Libris 336416
1 CONT ^ Swedish Diplomatarium's main catalog of medieval letters no. 245
1 CONT ^ Harrison, Dick (2002). The earl's century : a story of 13th-century Sweden . Stockholm: Ordfront. Libris 8449618 . ISBN 91-7324-898-3
1 CONT ^ [ a b c ] Gillingstam, Hans :  Folkungaätten in Swedish biographical dictionary (1964-1966)
1 CONT ^ Theutenberg, Bo J. "VÄSTERGOTLAND LAGMÄN - ALGOTSSONER AND FOLKUNGAR" . Archived from the original on 7 March 2016 . Read April 30, 2015 .
1 CONT ^ Toll, Hans. "People earl" . Swedish biographical lexicon . Read March 18, 2014 .
1 CONT ^ Toll, Hans, Hans. "King Swears the Other's Queens" . The ancient friend 197-206 . Read March 18, 2014 .
1 CONT Sources 
1 CONT Beckman, Nat.:  Birger Brosa in Swedish biographical dictionary (1924), volume 4
1 CONT Harrison, Dick (2002). The earl's century : a story of 13th-century Sweden . Stockholm: Ordfront. Libris 8449618 . ISBN 91-7324-898-3
