0 @NFS5270@ NOTE === This individual was passed on to me some ===
1 CONT This individual was passed on to me some time ago as being part of the family pedigree.  It was not until recently that I really stopped to pay attention to this part of the family line.  I have found that there are several of the pedigree that ar
1 CONC e not "connected" to the mail family line.  Kionick Gundersson has a direct connection but Haakon and other related individuals do not have a continuous connection.  It is not known if some family member who may have done this part of the genealogi
1 CONC cal line assumed that because Kionick Gundersson was related continuously that other royalty may fall into line at some point.  As I have found some documented information on this person (Eufemia) I have contained the infomration in the notes secti
1 CONC on of his/her pedigree. Eufemia In June 1294 King Edward I of England sent a letter to Duke Håkon. King Edward expressed his happiness about the recent message from Norway that Håkon wanted to take an English wife. Håkon's English adventure cam
1 CONC e to nothing, however. The reason being that King Eirik and his advisor, Audun Hugleiksson, thought France a better ally in the war that flared up between England and France. Baron Audun accordingly traveled to Paris, where he arranged for Håkon t
1 CONC o marry Isabella of Joigny, a relative of the French king. This marriage also came to naught. The reason this time being Baron Audun's broken promises regarding aid for France in its war against England. **Finally, Håkon was engaged to Eufemia, da
1 CONC ughter of a count from Rügen. In 1299 Eufemia and Håkon were married in Oslo, and Håkon's gift to Eufemia was Hovedøya, the green little island just off the city. A few months later Eufemia found herself Queen of Norway, as her husband was appo
1 CONC inted king after the death of King Eirik, his brother. We don't know when Eufemia was born, but there is reason to believe that she was somewhat older than Håkon. Despite the marriage being a political affair, the couple seemed to be close. Håko
1 CONC n only fathered one  child out of wedlock, an unusually low number for a medieval king. Their daughter, Ingebjørg, was born in 1301, but she was to stay a single child. The failure to produce a male heir was a grave problem for the kingdom. **A
1 CONC s a person Eufemia must have been very different from Håkon. While he was a learned, ascetic and deeply religious man; she brought music, song, and literature from her home in Germany. And through the so-called Eufemia-visene she introduced romant
1 CONC ic courtly fiction into the Scandinavian countries. There are three works in Swedish: Ivan Lejonriddaren, Hertig Fredrik av Normandie and Flores och Blanzeflor. Eufemia-visene were probably done in Swedish to please Duke Erik, who wanted little Ing
1 CONC ebjørg's hand in marriage. Here are the first lines of Flores och Blanzeflor: Som iak ij bøkir skrifuith sa ok æwintyrith sigher ij fra, een hedhin konung foor medh brand hæria ok ødha sancti Iacobs land. Han var een formæktigh man; Fenix af
1 CONC f Apolis swa heet han. Tha thera skip varo komin swa nær, the lagdho til land ok dwaldis ther om siæx vikur tima sirla ok swa snima.  (side 2)  Huath the matto aff landith køra, the loto thet alt til skipin føra, swa at ther nær stodh aldre tr
1 CONC æ, a thrætighi mila ey folk ey fæ æller nakath thet hws folk matte ij vara; thet hafdho the forøøt swa sara. Translated As I in books saw written and adventure tells about an ancient king came with fire sacked and destroyed St Jacob's land. H
1 CONC e was a mighty man; Fenix of Apolis was his name. When their ships were coming close, they went ashore and stayed there for about six weeks both night and day.  (page 2)  The riches of the land they had taken, they carried everything to the ships
1 CONC , so that not a tree stood there, for thirty miles no man or animal or any shelter of any kind; they had destroyed everything. Eufemia-visene were Romans, a kind of narrative poetry that first appeared in France in the twelfth century. Romans wer
1 CONC e written in the "Romance" language, that is  French, and were stories about knights and their ladies, about dragons and adventure. Typical examples are the stories of King Arthur and the Knights of the Round Table.**Stralsund was the most importan
1 CONC t city of the Rügen princes. At this time Stralsund was growing fast. Beautiful churches were built, as well as town halls and monasteries. The city grew rich on trade and shipping, and it became one of the most important cities of the Hansa. With
1 CONC out doubt Eufemia brought many impulses from her hometown with her into the Norwegian court. Moreover, her father - Prince Witzlaw - was one of King Håkon's closest friends. The prince spent his last years in Oslo, where he died in 1302. **Queen E
1 CONC ufemia lived to see her eleven-year-old daughter married to Duke Erik, before she died on May 1 1312. She was buried in the Church of St Mary in Oslo.
