0 @NFS5551@ NOTE === Was Baron Gaut Gautsen the Father of Sigurd Torsnes? ===
1 CONT There is sufficient and credible evidence to suggest that Sigurd Gautsen is NOT the same person as Sigurd Guttormsen, and that Sigurd Guttormsen was NOT the son of Gaut Gautsen but the son of Guttorm Kalvsen Torsnes. The central piece of primary-so
1 CONC urce evidence is a 1409 diploma (DN XXI, #249), where Bård and Guttorm Sigurdsen provide their ancestry in order to prove their claim to timber rights at Knarrevik forest, part of the great landholdings of the Torsnes people. Their genealogy is co
1 CONC rroborated, according to the diploma, by people from and around Torsnes, who state the genealogy reflects what they know to be true, and which was told to them by their elders. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT From the 1409 diploma we find the following line of descent for Bård and Guttorm Sigurdsen:
1 CONT  
1 CONT 1.	Viking ‘The Rich’ på Torsnes
1 CONT 2.	Inga Vikingsdatter Torsnes (Viking’s daughter, who marries Kalv, about whom we know nothing else)
1 CONT 3.	Guttorm Kalvsen Torsnes
1 CONT 4.	**Sigurd Guttormsen Torsnes**
1 CONT 5.	Guttorm Sigurdsen Torsnes
1 CONT 6.	Sigurd Guttormsen Torsnes 
1 CONT 7.	Bård and Guttorm Sigurdsen Torsnes 
1 CONT Sigurd Torsnes appears in 9 diplomas from the 1300s, named as Sigurd paa Thorsnes, Sigurd Galte or just plain Sigurd. He is never referred to by a patronym, which has enabled much debate.
1 CONT 
1 CONT There is a Sigurd Gautsen active in the period. He seems to only appear in diplomas (3 of them) with his full name: Sigurd Gautsen, and never with an ætt or farm name (never, then, as Sigurd Gautsen Torsnes, Sigurd Gautsen Galte, etc).
1 CONT 
1 CONT Norwegian historians and genealogists have been debating the history of the Torsnes people for nearly 100 years, with arguments dating back to at least Solleid (1930), who theorized that the owners of Torsnes descend from Gaut på Ænes/Mel. Sollei
1 CONC d’s theory was picked up by Høyland (1939), and also appears to be the basis for Galtung’s work (1948).
1 CONT 
1 CONT New research starting in the 1950s, however, offer an alternative theory. Dahl (1950), for example, uses the 1409 diploma (DN XXI, #249) to argue that the ancestry of the Torsnes people can be traced to Viking på Torsnes (c1190- ) and his daughte
1 CONC r Inga Vikingsdatter. As far as I can tell, most Norwegian researchers have shifted to Dahl’s theory, including Losnegård (2003), who wrote the bygdebok for Jondal kommune. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT see the document in Memories for additional discussion of the current research.
