0 @NFS5634@ NOTE === Who Was Sigurd Galte Torsnes ===
1 CONT There are two competing theories regarding the ancestry of Sigurd Galte (aka Galtung--but that ætt is used by a descendant in the 1600s). The first, seems to originate (as far as I can tell) with the work of Sollied (1930) and developed further b
1 CONC y R. Høyland (1939); they propose that Sigurd Galte is the son of Gaut 'Unge' Gautsen, the son of Gaut Jonsen, the son of Jon Gautsen, the son of Gaut på Ænes/Ornes. The second major theory (which started with Dahl (1950) and further substantiat
1 CONC ed by others (including Harstad 2014) is that Sigurd Guttormsen is the son of Guttorm Kalvsen, the son of Kalv på Torsnes & Inga Vikingsdatter Torsnes, who was the daughter of Viking 'Audghe' på Torsnes. This ancestry is attested to in a primar
1 CONC y source, a 1409 diploma (DN XXI #249) where witnesses confirm this is the ancestry of Bård & Guttorm Sigurdsen who are the primary inheritors of Torsnes when their father Sigurd Guttormsen (who was the son of Guttorm Sigurdsen). 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Based on the available evidence, arguments, and historical documentation, the latter theory is the one most likely to be correct (see document on this posted in Memories). Høyland's theory (and those that follow Høyland, e.g., Galtung 1948) is mo
1 CONC re based on assumptions about the early generations than primary documentation (neither Høyland nor Galtung reference the 1409 diploma, for example). 
1 CONT 
1 CONT In reconfiguring the lines I have tried to preserve both theories, at least until we can agree one or the other. But lines of ancestry are filled with inaccurate assumptions & claims, which I have tried either to flag, correct, or otherwise revise.
0 @NFS5635@ NOTE === Children of Sigurd Guttomsem & Sigrid Guttormsdatter ===
1 CONT We know, with strong certainty (based on the 1409 diploma (DN XXI, #249)), of one child: Guttorm Sigurdsen. But Guttorm appears in the 1409 diploma soley because Bård & Guttorm Sigurdsen are his direct descendants, and they were trying to prove th
1 CONC eir ownership rights to timber based on their ancestry. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT The following are the likely other children, all of whom of backed by historical genealogical research, though some are perhaps more theoretical (especially Brynhild & Hallbjørn) than others:
1 CONT Viking Sigurdsen Torsnes (appears in a diploma as an owner of a portion of Torsnes in the 1340s)
1 CONT Erik Sigurdsen Torsnes (Harstad 2014 & others; moved to Aske in Mosterøy) 
1 CONT Brynhild Sigurdsdatter (proposed by Dahl 1950; went to Rogne i Voss parish)
1 CONT Hallbjørn Sigurdsen (proposed by Dahl 1950; becomes associated with sister's farm in Rogne)
1 CONT 
1 CONT No other children have been identified by the main streams of historical research, so any others much be treated with skepticism unless further documentation beyond other trees is found.
