0 @NFS5782@ NOTE === Merchant ===
1 CONT Mogens (Magnus) was a merchant in Bergen, a sheriff in the Faroe Islands, a Danish and Dutch war captain.  Employed by King Fredrik II as "hijacker" to drive pirates away from Norwegian waters.  Seduced his sister-in-law Margrethe (c. 1580) after "
1 CONC abducting" her, and Margrethe gave birth to a child, who died.  He was sentenced to death for piracy, instigated by his friend, interest chief and former lord of the house at Bergenshus, Christoffer Walkendorf.  The sentence was later rendered dea
1 CONC d and powerless, and the body was dug up and given a worthy burial.
0 @NFS5783@ NOTE === Life story ===
1 CONT Magnus or Mogens became, for example, in the Faroe Islands approx.  1545 by Norwegian parents.  He became a good sailor early on and as a young man was often in Bergen where he became acquainted with the Gyntersberg family and especially the daught
1 CONC er in the house, Margaretha.  Maqnus was early master of a ship between Bergen and the Faroe Islands, which belonged to his relatives in Bergen.  He excelled both at home and in Dutch service.  At that time, the North Sea was particularly trouble
1 CONC d by the English and French pirates, and the Danish / Norwegian King Fredrik II sent Magnus there to keep the sea tidy.  As a salary, he should have half of the broken-up ships w / his value.  The rest fell to the king, but it turned out that the k
1 CONC ing did not get everything he was entitled to under the agreement.  Magnus at last managed to talk away from these accusations.  There was a complaint about Magnus and his crew, from the Faroe Islands, about excursions of many kinds.  Because of th
1 CONC e usefulness the king had of Magnus, this was thrown down.  Shortly afterwards Magnus, who was then a citizen of Bergen, made an attempt to find Greenland at his own expense.  Whether this is successful is doubtful, but Magnus claimed that he had g
1 CONC ot Greenland "in sight and visibility".  Ashore he did not come because of storm, fog, drift ice and strong current.  Shortly after returning home, Magnus married a daughter of Axel Gyntersberg.  Not Margaret, but Sofie.  This led to a reaction fro
1 CONC m Margaret who claimed that Magnus had gotten her to bed under the promise of marriage.  This became a scandal and Magnus sued Margaret and her husband for false charges and they were placed in custody for a while.  After a long process, they wer
1 CONC e acquitted.  As a reward for his Greenland journey, Magnus together with a couple of merchants got the exclusive right to trade with the Faroe Islands for 5 years, but p.g.a.  accusations of fraud and fraud by former co-workers in Bergen he was di
1 CONC smissed from this court and had to pay damages.  He then went with his ship to the Netherlands where he served with the Statholder Prince Moritz of Orange to hijack ships that did not pay their taxes to the Netherlands to cross the coast.  He hijac
1 CONC ked a large English ship that was judged in the Netherlands and sold and brought to Bergen.
1 CONT  Queen Elisabeth complained to the Danish Government and Magnus who now lived in Danmar was accused of piracy.  King Frederick II was now dead and the Custodian Government (in King Christian IV's minor) took the matter very seriously.  Magnus was i
1 CONC mprisoned, shortly afterwards sentenced to death and beheaded at Slottsparken in Copenhagen on 18 January 1589.  Then the sharpener would blindfold, Magnus said.  "No, I've seen so many dragged Klinge before, I'm not scared, don't you either".
1 CONT  Now it turned out that the trial had some formal weaknesses and the following year declared Lord's Day, the judgment invalid and allowed the widow "to honor the Body honorably."
