0 @NFS5967@ NOTE === Karl Knutsson Bonde ===
1 CONT Karl Knutsson var den andre kungen med namnet Karl och således Karl II och inte Karl VIII som tillkom efter en kungalängd som sägs ha manipulerats på 1500-talet. Karl Knutssons grav ligger i Vadstena och på gravhällen anges Karl II. Karl Knut
1 CONC sson kallade sig själv aldrig för Bonde.
0 @NFS5968@ NOTE === Life History ===
1 CONT Born around 1408 into the Bonde family, a wealthy and influential noble house
1 CONT 	•	Not royal by blood → this is important
1 CONT 	•	Rose through:
1 CONT 	•	Military leadership
1 CONT 	•	Political alliances
1 CONT 	•	Anti-Danish sentiment among Swedish nobles
1 CONT 
1 CONT By the 1440s, he was already one of the most powerful men in Sweden.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ⸻
1 CONT 
1 CONT Becoming King (1448)
1 CONT 
1 CONT When King Christopher of Bavaria (Union king) died in 1448:
1 CONT 	•	Sweden refused to accept another Danish-dominated ruler
1 CONT 	•	Karl was elected King of Sweden by the Swedish council
1 CONT 	•	Shortly after, he also claimed the title King of Norway (briefly)
1 CONT 
1 CONT This was huge because:
1 CONT 	•	He was the first Swedish king chosen from the nobility, not inherited royalty
1 CONT 	•	It challenged the very structure of the Kalmar Union
1 CONT 
1 CONT ⸻
1 CONT 
1 CONT First Reign (1448–1457): Power & Pushback
1 CONT 
1 CONT Karl ruled as a strong, centralized monarch, but:
1 CONT 	•	He raised taxes to strengthen the crown
1 CONT 	•	Took land and power from rival nobles
1 CONT 	•	Alienated both:
1 CONT 	•	Union supporters
1 CONT 	•	Other Swedish aristocrats
1 CONT 
1 CONT Denmark seized this opportunity.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1457, Danish forces and Swedish unionists overthrew him.
1 CONT ➡️ Karl fled to Danzig (modern Gdańsk).
1 CONT 
1 CONT ⸻
1 CONT 
1 CONT Exile & Political Survival
1 CONT 
1 CONT Even in exile:
1 CONT 	•	Karl didn’t give up
1 CONT 	•	Maintained contacts with Swedish nobles
1 CONT 	•	Waited for Danish authority to weaken
1 CONT 
1 CONT This patience paid off.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ⸻
1 CONT 
1 CONT Second Reign (1464–1465): Brief Return
1 CONT 	•	Anti-Danish uprisings erupted in Sweden
1 CONT 	•	Karl was invited back and restored as king
1 CONT 
1 CONT But:
1 CONT 	•	Power struggles resumed immediately
1 CONT 	•	Swedish bishops and nobles turned against him
1 CONT 	•	He was deposed again after barely a year
1 CONT 
1 CONT This highlights a key truth:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Karl wasn’t just fighting Denmark — he was also fighting Sweden’s own elite.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ⸻
1 CONT 
1 CONT Third Reign (1467–1470): Final Rule
1 CONT 
1 CONT After years of chaos:
1 CONT 	•	Karl returned once more to the throne in 1467
1 CONT 	•	This time, his position was stronger
1 CONT 	•	He ruled until his death in 1470
1 CONT 
1 CONT Though still controversial, he managed more stability than before.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ⸻
1 CONT 
1 CONT Leadership Style & Personality
1 CONT 
1 CONT Karl was:
1 CONT 	•	Ambitious
1 CONT 	•	Stubborn
1 CONT 	•	Politically shrewd
1 CONT 	•	Willing to rule forcefully
1 CONT 
1 CONT Strengths:
1 CONT 	•	Asserted Swedish independence
1 CONT 	•	Broke dynastic tradition
1 CONT 	•	Strengthened royal authority
1 CONT 
1 CONT Weaknesses:
1 CONT 	•	Poor at compromise
1 CONT 	•	Created powerful enemies
1 CONT 	•	Often ruled through pressure rather than unity
1 CONT 
1 CONT ⸻
1 CONT 
1 CONT Death & Legacy
1 CONT 	•	Died in 1470
1 CONT 	•	Buried in Riddarholmen Church, Stockholm
1 CONT 	•	His death didn’t end the struggle—but shaped it
