0 @NFS6139@ NOTE === svensk krönika by Olaus Petrisvensk krönika by Olaus Petri ===
1 CONT En svensk krönika by Olaus Petri is a historical chronicle that provides insights into Sweden’s medieval history, particularly the royal succession and political struggles of the time.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The passage you referenced describes Valdemar Birgersson’s rise to the throne in 1250, following the death of his uncle Erik Eriksson. It highlights his powerful lineage, tracing his ancestry to four influential families, including the House of S
1 CONC verker and House of Bjälbo. The text also mentions his coronation at Linköping Cathedral in 1251, which was recorded by Olaus Petri.
1 CONT Summary of Additional Sections
1 CONT Royal Elections: The chronicle describes how Swedish kings were chosen, often through a mix of hereditary claims and noble support. It highlights the role of Linköping Cathedral in coronation ceremonies.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Conflicts Between Dynasties: Olaus Petri details the power struggles between the House of Sverker and the House of Bjälbo, emphasizing battles like Gestilren.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Church and Monarchy: The text explores the relationship between Swedish rulers and the Church, including how religious leaders influenced political decisions.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Valdemar Birgersson’s Downfall: The chronicle recounts Valdemar’s conflicts with his brothers, particularly Magnus Ladulås, who ultimately overthrew him.
1 CONT MS COPILOT AI 15/05/2025
0 @NFS6140@ NOTE === the House of Bjälbo (Bjälboätten) AKA the House of Folkung Folkungaätten ===
1 CONT Valdemar Birgersson, also known as Waldemar in English, was a Swedish king from the House of Bjälbo (Bjälboätten), also referred to as the House of Folkung. His full appellation in historical records is often Valdemar Birgersson of Bjälbo, ackn
1 CONC owledging his noble lineage.
1 CONT The House of Bjälbo[a], also known as the House of Folkung[b] (Swedish: Bjälboätten or Folkungaätten), was a Swedish family that produced several medieval bishops, jarls and kings of Sweden.[2] It also provided three kings of Norway and one kin
1 CONC g of Denmark in the 14th century.
1 CONT The house has been known as the "House of Folkung" since the 17th century, and this name is still commonly used in Swedish works of reference. The name "Folkung" does appear as early as in 12th century sources, but is then usually not applied to me
1 CONC mbers of the family.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In an effort to avoid confusion with the Folkunge Party some modern historians have argued that "House of Bjälbo" would be a better name because Birger Jarl lived there and it is the family's oldest known manor. Bjälbo is located in Östergötlan
1 CONC d, outside of Skänninge in the present-day municipality of Mjölby. In any case the members of this dynasty never used a name to refer to themselves since family names were not widely adopted in Sweden until the 16th century, thus neither name i
1 CONC s more "correct" for the dynasty, apart from the potential for confusion.[1]
1 CONT Around 1100, Folke the Fat became the first known Bjälbo jarl, and probably the first jarl of all Sweden, under king Inge I of Sweden. He was married to a Ingegerd Knutsdotter of Denmark, daughter of king Canute IV of Denmark. According to legends
1 CONC , he was the first of his family so elevated. Nothing is known of any of Folke's collateral relatives, though it is well-established that several of his sons' descendants were important lords.
1 CONT The term Folkung originally referred to a political faction in medieval Sweden that opposed centralized royal power. However, until the 17th century, the name was used only in this political sense.
1 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Bj%C3%A4lbo
1 CONT [1] Folkungaätten (Nordisk familjebok. 1908)
1 CONT [2] Folkungaätten (Samlaren / Ny följd. Årgång 10. 1929)
1 CONT 
1 CONT In the 17th and 18th centuries, Swedish historians began applying the name Folkung (or Folkungaätten) to the noble House of Bjälbo, which had produced several Swedish kings. This retroactive naming led to some confusion, as the medieval Folkung
1 CONC s were not necessarily direct descendants of Jarl Folke the Fat, the supposed ancestor of the Bjälbo family1.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Despite this historical distinction, modern Swedes often use both Bjälboätten and Folkungaätten interchangeably when referring to the dynasty. It’s a fascinating example of how historical narratives evolve over time!
1 CONT [1] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Folkung
0 @NFS6141@ NOTE === Familjeförhållande ENGLISH Family Relations ===
1 CONT Han efterträdde sin morbror Erik Eriksson som kung 1250, alltså som minderårig, eftersom han härstammade från fyra mäktiga familjer. Hans farfar var Magnus Minnesköld, som stupade i slaget vid Gestilren, ett av slagen mot Sverkerska ätten
1 CONC . Hans farmor var Ingrid Ylva, som möjligen var sondotter till Sverker den äldre. Hans morfar var kung Erik Knutsson av Sverige och hans mormor Rikissa av Danmark, dotter till Valdemar den store.
1 CONT Valdemar kröntes till kung vid elva års ålder. Det skedde i Linköpings domkyrka år 1251. Olaus Petri beskrev kungavalet i En svensk krönika: 
1 CONT English translation:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Family Relations He succeeded his uncle, Erik Eriksson, as king in 1250, while still a minor, as he descended from four powerful families. His grandfather was Magnus Minnesköld, who fell in the Battle of Gestilren, one of the conflicts against th
1 CONC e House of Sverker. His grandmother was Ingrid Ylva, who may have been the granddaughter of Sverker the Elder. His maternal grandfather was King Erik Knutsson of Sweden, and his maternal grandmother was Rikissa of Denmark, daughter of Valdemar th
1 CONC e Great.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Valdemar was crowned king at the age of eleven. The coronation took place in Linköping Cathedral in 1251. Olaus Petri described the royal election in A Swedish Chronicle.
0 @NFS6142@ NOTE ===  (1157) ===
